window.CONCEPTS = window.CONCEPTS || {};
(function(){
const R = String.raw;

/* ===================== MIDTERM ===================== */

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/map"] = [
  R`<b>Forward model.</b> $Y=AX+W$ with $W\sim N(0,\Lambda^{-1})$, so $p(y|x)=\dfrac{|\Lambda|^{1/2}}{(2\pi)^{M/2}}\exp\{-\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2\}$ where $\|v\|_\Lambda^2=v^t\Lambda v$. $A$ is $M\times N$ ($M$ may be larger, equal or smaller than $N$); for i.i.d. noise of variance $\sigma^2$, $\Lambda=I/\sigma^2$ and $-\log p(y|x)=\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\|y-Ax\|^2+\text{const}$. (Lectures 12, 17)`,
  R`<b>ML estimate and why it fails.</b> $\hat x_{ML}=\arg\min_x\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2=(A^t\Lambda A)^{-1}A^t\Lambda y$ (for square nonsingular $A$ simply $A^{-1}y$). With the SVD $A=U\Sigma V^t$, $\hat X_{ML}=X+V\Sigma^{-1}\tilde W$: the noise is amplified by $1/\sigma_i$, so the estimate is useless when the condition number $\kappa(A)=\sigma_1/\sigma_N$ is large. Even $A=I$ gives $\hat x=y$: the real problem is that ML uses no image model. (Lecture 12)`,
  R`<b>MAP = data term + prior term.</b> $\hat x_{MAP}=\arg\max_x\log p(x|y)=\arg\min_x\{-\log p(y|x)-\log p(x)\}$; $\log p(y)$ is dropped because it does not depend on $x$. The three steps are: choose the forward model, choose the prior, minimize the cost. Cost functions pick the estimator: zero-one cost gives MAP (posterior mode), squared error gives MMSE $E[X|Y]$, absolute error gives the conditional median. (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>GMRF prior and quadratic cost.</b> $p(x)=\dfrac{|B|^{1/2}}{(2\pi)^{N/2}}\exp\{-\tfrac12x^tBx\}$ with precision $B_{i,j}=\frac{1}{\sigma_x^2}(\delta_{i-j}-g_{i-j})$ ($g$ = noncausal prediction filter). The MAP cost is $f(x)=\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\|y-Ax\|^2+\tfrac12x^tBx$, a quadratic in $x$; the prior term is what regularizes the inversion.`,
  R`<b>Closed form by completing the square.</b> $\log p(x|y)=-\tfrac12(x-\mu)^tR^{-1}(x-\mu)+c(y)$ with posterior precision $R^{-1}=\frac{1}{\sigma^2}A^tA+B$ (data precision plus prior precision) and $\mu(y)=(A^tA+\sigma^2B)^{-1}A^ty$. $A^tA+\sigma^2B$ is positive definite, hence invertible even when $A^tA$ is singular. With general $\Lambda$: $\hat x=(A^t\Lambda A+B)^{-1}A^t\Lambda y$; equivalently set $\nabla f=0$. (Lecture 12)`,
  R`<b>MAP = MMSE for Gaussians.</b> The posterior is Gaussian, hence symmetric and unimodal, so mean = mode = median and $\hat x_{MMSE}=\hat x_{MAP}=\mu(y)$. This fails for non-Gaussian (edge-preserving) priors, which is why later an MSE-trained denoiser approximates the conditional mean, not the MAP denoiser.`,
  R`<b>Limits of the regularization parameter.</b> $\lambda=\sigma^2/\sigma_x^2$ is the noise-to-signal ratio. As $\lambda\to0$ (flat prior, $\beta\to0$) the estimate tends to $\hat x_{ML}$; as $\lambda\to\infty$ the prior dominates and $\hat x$ collapses to the prior mode (zero for a proper zero-mean GMRF; a constant image if the row sums of $B$ are zero). With no blur ($A=I$) the ICD update is the weighted average $x_s\leftarrow\dfrac{y_s+\lambda\sum_{r\in\partial s}g_{s-r}x_r}{1+\lambda}$. (Lecture 14)`,
  R`<b>Gradient and Hessian.</b> $\nabla f(x)=-\frac{1}{\sigma^2}A^t(y-Ax)+Bx$ and $\nabla^2f=\frac{1}{\sigma^2}A^tA+B$. Sign pitfall: the gradient of $\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|^2$ is $-A^t(y-Ax)=A^t(Ax-y)$. The notes scale by $\sigma^2$ and write $H\triangleq A^tA+\sigma^2B$, so $\sigma^2\nabla f(x)=Hx-A^ty$.`,
  R`<b>Gradient descent pseudo-code.</b> Initialize $x^{(0)}$; repeat $x^{(k+1)}=x^{(k)}+\alpha\,[A^t(y-Ax^{(k)})-\sigma^2Bx^{(k)}]$, i.e. $x^{(k+1)}=(I-\alpha H)x^{(k)}+\alpha A^ty$. Nothing is inverted: each iteration applies $A$, $A^t$ and $B$, which for an LSI blur are three convolutions ($a_s$, $a_{-s}$, $\delta_s-g_s$). (Lecture 13)`,
  R`<b>Convergence of gradient descent.</b> The error $\epsilon^{(k)}=x^{(k)}-x^{(\infty)}$ obeys $\epsilon^{(k+1)}=(I-\alpha H)\epsilon^{(k)}$, so in the eigenbasis of $H=E\Lambda E^t$, $\tilde\epsilon_i^{(k)}=(1-\alpha h_i)^k\tilde\epsilon_i^{(0)}$. Stability needs $|1-\alpha h_i|&lt;1$ for all $i$, i.e. $0&lt;\alpha&lt;2/h_{\max}$; with $\alpha=1/h_{\max}$ the slowest mode decays as $(1-1/\kappa(H))^k$. In frequency, $h(\omega)=|a(\omega)|^2+\lambda(1-g(\omega))$ is low-pass, so high frequencies (edges) converge last. (Lecture 13)`,
  R`<b>Line search.</b> $d^{(k)}=-\sigma^2\nabla f(x^{(k)})$, $\alpha^{(k)}=\arg\min_{\alpha\ge0}f(x^{(k)}+\alpha d^{(k)})$, $x^{(k+1)}=x^{(k)}+\alpha^{(k)}d^{(k)}$. Solve $[\nabla f(x+\alpha d)]^td=0$: for the quadratic cost $\alpha^{(k)}=\dfrac{(y-Ax)^tAd-\sigma^2x^tBd}{\|d\|_H^2}$, which for the gradient direction is $\|d\|^2/\|d\|_H^2$. The cost is monotone decreasing and no step-size parameter is needed. (Lecture 14)`,
  R`<b>ICD update, derived.</b> Take $d=\varepsilon_s$ (one pixel) and solve $\partial f(x+\alpha\varepsilon_s)/\partial\alpha=0$: $\alpha^*=\dfrac{(y-Ax)^tA_{*,s}-\sigma^2x^tB_{*,s}}{\|A_{*,s}\|^2+\sigma^2B_{s,s}}$. Writing out the GMRF prior, $x_s\leftarrow x_s+\dfrac{(y-Ax)^tA_{*,s}-\lambda\big(x_s-\sum_{r\in\partial s}g_{s-r}x_r\big)}{\|A_{*,s}\|^2+\lambda}$: data mismatch versus deviation from the neighbor prediction, weighted by $\lambda$. (Lecture 14)`,
  R`<b>Fast ICD pseudo-code.</b> Initialize $e\leftarrow y-Ax$. For $K$ iterations, for each pixel $s$: $v\leftarrow x_s$; $x_s\leftarrow x_s+\dfrac{e^tA_{*,s}-\lambda(x_s-\sum_{r\in\partial s}g_{s-r}x_r)}{\|A_{*,s}\|^2+\lambda}$; $e\leftarrow e-A_{*,s}(x_s-v)$. Keeping the residual as a state variable means a pixel update costs only the number of nonzeros in column $A_{*,s}$. Positivity is enforced by clipping $\alpha^*\ge-x_s$.`,
  R`<b>ICD versus gradient descent.</b> ICD is Gauss–Seidel: with $H=L+D+L^t$ the error obeys $\epsilon^{(k+1)}=-(L+D)^{-1}L^t\epsilon^{(k)}$, and in frequency the rate is $P(\omega)=\big|L^*(\omega)/(L(\omega)+h_0)\big|$. ICD converges fast at high frequencies (edges first) and slower at low ones, the opposite of gradient descent; in Example 5.1/5.2 gradient descent needs 80+ iterations, ICD under 20. Initialize ICD with a good low-frequency estimate. Conjugate gradient uses $H$-conjugate directions and is exact in at most $N$ steps for a quadratic. (Lecture 14)`,
  R`<b>General Gibbs prior.</b> For any prior $p(x)=\frac1z\exp\{-u(x)\}$ the MAP cost is $\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+u(x)$; the partition function is a constant and never enters the optimization. Pairwise form: $u(x)=\sum_{\{s,r\}\in P}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)$, which for $\rho(\Delta)=\Delta^2/2$ recovers the GMRF via $x^tBx=\sum_sa_sx_s^2+\sum_{\{s,r\}}b_{s,r}(x_s-x_r)^2$. (Lecture 15)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/prox"] = [
  R`<b>Definition.</b> The proximal map of a proper closed convex $u$ is $H(y)=\arg\min_{x\in\Omega}\{\tfrac12\|y-x\|^2+u(x)\}$; with a scale, $F(v)=\arg\min_x\{f(x)+\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\|x-v\|^2\}$. The objective is strictly convex, so $H(y)$ exists and is unique for every $y$. Picture: a stake at $y$, a spring with unit constant, and a cart that would roll down $u$; the cart stops where the hill force balances the spring force. (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>MAP-denoiser interpretation.</b> $H(y)$ is the MAP estimate for $Y=X+W$, $W\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, prior $p(x)\propto e^{-h(x)}$: so a given $H$ implies the forward model "AWGN of variance $\sigma^2$" and the prior $e^{-h}$. Dually, $F(v)$ is the MAP estimate with likelihood $e^{-f}$ and Gaussian prior $X\sim N(v,\sigma^2I)$. The proximal map converts an <em>objective</em> into an <em>action</em>. (Lecture 26)`,
  R`<b>Repeated application.</b> $y^{(k+1)}=H(y^{(k)})$ converges to $\arg\min_xu(x)$ (chapter problem). This has exactly the structure of the augmented Lagrangian dual recursion $\lambda^{(k+1)}=\arg\max_\lambda\{g(\lambda)-\frac{1}{2a}\|\lambda-\lambda^{(k)}\|^2\}$ with $u=-g$, which is why that recursion converges to $\lambda^*$. (Lectures 22, 23)`,
  R`<b>Quadratic data term.</b> For $f(x)=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2$ set $-A^t\Lambda(y-Ax)+\frac{1}{\sigma^2}(x-v)=0$ to get $F(v)=\big(A^t\Lambda A+\tfrac{1}{\sigma^2}I\big)^{-1}\big(A^t\Lambda y+\tfrac{1}{\sigma^2}v\big)=v+\big(\tfrac{1}{\sigma^2}I+A^t\Lambda A\big)^{-1}A^t\Lambda(y-Av)$. In the ADMM scaling $a=1/\sigma^2$: $F(z)=(A^tA/\sigma_y^2+aI)^{-1}(A^ty/\sigma_y^2+az)$.`,
  R`<b>Shrinkage (soft threshold).</b> For $u(x)=\lambda\|x\|_1$ the objective is separable; per component, $\arg\min_{x_i}\{\tfrac12(y_i-x_i)^2+\lambda|x_i|\}$ is $y_i-\lambda$ for $y_i>\lambda$, $0$ for $|y_i|\le\lambda$, $y_i+\lambda$ for $y_i&lt;-\lambda$ (check the three cases: for $x_i>0$ the derivative $x_i-y_i+\lambda=0$, etc.). Compactly $S_\lambda(y)=\operatorname{sign}(y)\max\{|y|-\lambda,0\}$, applied componentwise. With $\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\|y-x\|^2+\lambda\|x\|_1$ the threshold becomes $\lambda\sigma^2$. (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Indicator functions and clipping.</b> $\tilde g(x)=0$ on a closed convex set $A$ and $\infty$ outside is proper, closed and convex; its proximal map is the projection $\operatorname{clip}\{y,A\}$. For positivity, $[\operatorname{clip}\{y,\mathbb R^{+N}\}]_i=\max(y_i,0)$. (Lecture 21)`,
  R`<b>Variable splitting and the augmented Lagrangian.</b> To minimize $f(x)+g(Bx)$, split: $\min f(x)+g(v)$ s.t. $Bx=v$. The scaled augmented Lagrangian is $f(x)+g(v)+\frac a2\|Bx-v+u\|^2$ with $u=\lambda/a$; the update $u\leftarrow u+(B\hat x-\hat v)$ accumulates the constraint violation and pushes it to zero exactly (a plain penalty never does). (Lectures 21, 22, 24)`,
  R`<b>ADMM pseudo-code.</b> Initialize $a>0$, $u\leftarrow0$, $\hat v\leftarrow0$. Repeat: $\hat x\leftarrow\arg\min_{x\in\Omega}\{f(x)+\frac a2\|Bx-(\hat v-u)\|^2\}$; $\hat v\leftarrow\arg\min_v\{g(v)+\frac a2\|v-(B\hat x+u)\|^2\}$; $u\leftarrow u+B\hat x-\hat v$. Three tricks: split the variable, apply the augmented Lagrangian, do only one pass of alternating minimization. With $B=I$ and $\sigma^2=1/a$ this reads $x\leftarrow F(v-u)$, $v\leftarrow H(x+u)$, $u\leftarrow u+(x-v)$. (Lecture 24)`,
  R`<b>Two standard instances.</b> Total variation $\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\|Bx\|_1$: the $x$-update is quadratic and $\hat v\leftarrow S_{1/a}(B\hat x+u)$. Positivity: $\hat v\leftarrow\operatorname{clip}\{\hat x+u,\mathbb R^{+N}\}$, and the $x$-update is unconstrained. Convergence (Boyd et al.): for closed proper convex $f,g$ with Slater's condition, the constraint residual $\to0$, the cost $\to p^*$, and $u^{(k)}\to\lambda^*/a$; $a$ does not affect convergence in theory but trades iterations against conditioning of the $x$-step. (Lecture 25)`,
  R`<b>Plug-and-play.</b> Replace $H$ by a trained denoiser $H_\theta$. Virtual model $X=V+W$, $W\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$: take clean images $V_n$, add synthetic noise to get $X_n$, and minimize $L(\theta)=\sum_n\|V_n-H_\theta(X_n)\|^2$. The MSE loss yields (approximately) the MMSE denoiser $E[V|X]$, not the MAP denoiser. Once $F$ or $H$ is a general agent the iteration no longer minimizes any cost ("plug-and-pray"). (Lecture 26)`,
  R`<b>Moreau's theorem.</b> $H$ is the proximal map of a proper closed convex function iff $H$ is nonexpansive and conservative ($H=\nabla\phi$), which forces a self-adjoint gradient $\nabla H(x)=[\nabla H(x)]^t$ (for $H(x)=Ax$: $A=A^t$). A DNN denoiser almost never satisfies this, so it is not a proximal map. (Lecture 26)`,
  R`<b>Consensus equilibrium.</b> At convergence $x=v$, so $x^*=F(x^*-u^*)=H(x^*+u^*)$. If $F,H$ are proximal maps of convex $f,h$: $x^*$ minimizes $f(x)+\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\|x-(x^*-u^*)\|^2$ so $\nabla f(x^*)+u^*/\sigma^2=0$, and similarly $\nabla h(x^*)-u^*/\sigma^2=0$; adding gives $\nabla f(x^*)+\nabla h(x^*)=0$, hence $x^*$ is the MAP estimate and $u^*=-\sigma^2\nabla f(x^*)$ is the "noise" $H$ removes and $F$ re-injects. For general agents the CE equations are a force balance with no underlying cost. (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Fixed-point form.</b> Let $w_1=x-u$, $w_2=x+u$. The CE equations become $F(w_1^*)=H(w_2^*)=\tfrac12(w_1^*+w_2^*)$, i.e. $(2F-I)w_1^*=w_2^*$ and $(2H-I)w_2^*=w_1^*$, so $Tw_1^*=w_1^*$ with $T=(2H-I)(2F-I)$. Recover $x^*=\tfrac12(w_1^*+w_2^*)=F(w_1^*)$ and $u^*=\tfrac12(w_2^*-w_1^*)$. (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Contraction, nonexpansive, Mann iteration.</b> $T$ is a contraction if $\|Tx-Ty\|\le L\|x-y\|$ with $L&lt;1$: a fixed point exists, is unique, and $x\leftarrow Tx$ converges (Banach). Nonexpansive ($L\le1$; rotations, reflections) does not guarantee convergence of $x\leftarrow Tx$, but the Mann iteration $w\leftarrow(1-\rho)w+\rho Tw$, $\rho\in(0,1)$, converges to a fixed point whenever one exists. Proximal maps are firmly nonexpansive ($\|Fx-Fy\|^2\le\langle Fx-Fy,x-y\rangle$), which makes $2F-I$ nonexpansive: expand $\|(2F-I)x-(2F-I)y\|^2=4\|Fx-Fy\|^2-4\langle Fx-Fy,x-y\rangle+\|x-y\|^2\le\|x-y\|^2$. Hence $T$ is nonexpansive and PnP-DR converges; $\rho=\tfrac12$ is exactly ADMM, which supplies the ADMM convergence proof. (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>MACE.</b> $K$ agents $F_k$ with weights $\mu_k$, $\sum_k\mu_k=1$. Stack $\mathbf F(w)=[F_1(w_1);\dots;F_K(w_K)]$ and let $G_\mu(w)$ replace every block by $\bar w=\sum_k\mu_kw_k$. MACE equations: $\mathbf F(w^*)=G_\mu(w^*)$, solution $x^*=\bar w^*$. Since $G_\mu G_\mu=G_\mu$, $(2G_\mu-I)^2=I$ and $w^*$ is a fixed point of $T=(2G_\mu-I)(2\mathbf F-I)$, solved by Mann iterations. With proximal agents the solution minimizes $\sum_k\mu_kf_k$; the weights retune regularization without retraining any agent. (Lecture 28)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/mm"] = [
  R`<b>Definition.</b> $q(x;x')$ is a surrogate (majorizer) for minimizing $f$ if $q(x';x')=f(x')$ (touches at the point of approximation) and $f(x)\le q(x;x')$ for all $x$ (lies above). Relaxed form used in the notes: $Q(x;x')$ is a surrogate if $f(x)\le Q(x;x')-Q(x';x')+f(x')$; a shift $c(x')$ does not change the minimizer. For maximization reverse the inequality. (Lecture 19)`,
  R`<b>Majorization–minimization.</b> $x^{(k+1)}=\arg\min_xQ(x;x^{(k)})$. The inequality chain $f(x^{(k+1)})\le q(x^{(k+1)};x^{(k)})\le q(x^{(k)};x^{(k)})=f(x^{(k)})$ (upper bound, minimization, tangency) shows the cost never increases. If $f$ is bounded below the sequence $f(x^{(k)})$ converges; the iterates $x^{(k)}$ themselves need not.`,
  R`<b>Five properties.</b> (1) Monotonicity: $Q(x;x')&lt;Q(x';x')\Rightarrow f(x)&lt;f(x')$, so any decrease of $Q$ helps. (2) Gradient: for differentiable $Q,f$ and $x'$ in the interior, $\nabla Q(x;x')|_{x=x'}=\nabla f(x')$ (tangency). (3) Fixed point: if $x^*$ minimizes $Q(\cdot;x^*)$ then $\nabla f(x^*)=0$, a global minimum if $f$ is convex. (4) Additivity: surrogates of summands add. (5) Composition: $Q(g(x);g(x'))$ is a surrogate for $f(g(x))$. (Lecture 19)`,
  R`<b>Strategy for the MAP cost.</b> $f(x)=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\sum_{\{s,r\}}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)$: the data term is already quadratic, so only $\rho$ needs a surrogate. Find a quadratic $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')$, then by additivity and composition ($\Delta=x_s-x_r$) $Q(x;x')=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\sum b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r;x'_s-x'_r)$ is a quadratic surrogate, i.e. a Chapter-5 GMRF problem each iteration. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Maximum-curvature method.</b> $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')=\rho'(\Delta')\Delta+\frac{c_{\max}}{2}(\Delta-\Delta')^2$ (plus a constant), with $a_1=\rho'(\Delta')$ from the gradient property and $c_{\max}=\max_\Delta\rho''(\Delta)$ to guarantee the upper bound. Simple ($c_{\max}$ fixed) but conservative, and unusable when $\rho''$ is unbounded near zero (total variation, GGMRF with $1\le p&lt;2$).`,
  R`<b>Symmetric-bound method.</b> Fit $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')=\frac{a_2}{2}\Delta^2$ (symmetric about 0, tangent at $\pm\Delta'$); matching gradients at $\Delta'$ gives $a_2=\rho'(\Delta')/\Delta'$, so $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')=\dfrac{\rho'(\Delta')}{2\Delta'}\Delta^2$ for $\Delta'\ne0$ and $\dfrac{\rho''(0)}{2}\Delta^2$ at $\Delta'=0$ (add the constant $\rho(\Delta')-\rho'(\Delta')\Delta'/2$ for exact tangency). Valid upper bound when $\rho$ is symmetric with $\rho'$ increasing near 0 and concave for $\Delta>0$ (equivalently $\rho'(\Delta)/\Delta$ non-increasing). Much tighter than maximum curvature, so faster algorithms. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Worked examples.</b> $\rho(\Delta)=|\Delta|$: $q(\Delta;\Delta')=\dfrac{\Delta^2}{2|\Delta'|}+\dfrac{|\Delta'|}{2}$ (check: equals $|\Delta'|$ at $\Delta'$, and $\frac{\Delta^2}{2|\Delta'|}+\frac{|\Delta'|}2-|\Delta|=\frac{(|\Delta|-|\Delta'|)^2}{2|\Delta'|}\ge0$). For $|\Delta-z|$ shift the variable. GGMRF $|\Delta|^p/p$: curvature $|\Delta'|^{p-2}$. Huber ($\Delta^2/2$ inside $T$, $T|\Delta|-T^2/2$ outside): $\rho'(\Delta')/\Delta'=\operatorname{clip}\{\Delta',[-T,T]\}/\Delta'=\min(1,T/|\Delta'|)$. Sums of such terms: majorize each term and add.`,
  R`<b>Reweighting view.</b> $\tilde b_{s,r}\leftarrow b_{s,r}\dfrac{\rho'(x'_s-x'_r)}{2(x'_s-x'_r)}$ and $Q(x;x')=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\sum_{\{s,r\}}\tilde b_{s,r}(x_s-x_r)^2$: each MM iteration is a GMRF MAP problem with recomputed pairwise weights (large differences get small weights, so edges are not smoothed). Huber: $\tilde b_{s,r}=b_{s,r}\operatorname{clip}\{x'_s-x'_r,[-T\sigma_x,T\sigma_x]\}/(2\sigma_x^2(x'_s-x'_r))$. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Surrogate line search.</b> $Q(x+\alpha d;x)=c+\theta_1\alpha+\tfrac12\theta_2\alpha^2$ with $\theta_1=-(y-Ax)^t\Lambda Ad+\sum_{\{s,r\}}2\tilde b_{s,r}(x_s-x_r)(d_s-d_r)$ and $\theta_2=d^tA^t\Lambda Ad+\sum_{\{s,r\}}2\tilde b_{s,r}(d_s-d_r)^2$, so $\alpha^*=\operatorname{clip}\{-\theta_1/\theta_2,[a,b]\}$ in one step, and $f(x+\alpha^*d)-f(x)\le Q(x+\alpha^*d;x)-Q(x;x)\le0$. Replaces the expensive half-interval rooting of $g(\alpha)=\theta_1+\theta_2\alpha+\sum b_{s,r}\rho'(\Delta x_{s,r}+\alpha\Delta d_{s,r})\Delta d_{s,r}$. (Lectures 18, 20)`,
  R`<b>ICD with majorized prior (pseudo-code).</b> $e\leftarrow y-Ax$; for each pixel $s$: $\tilde b_{s,r}\leftarrow b_{s,r}\rho'(x_s-x_r)/(2(x_s-x_r))$; $\theta_1\leftarrow-e^t\Lambda A_{*,s}+\sum_{r\in\partial s}2\tilde b_{s,r}(x_s-x_r)$; $\theta_2\leftarrow A_{*,s}^t\Lambda A_{*,s}+\sum_{r\in\partial s}2\tilde b_{s,r}$; $\alpha^*\leftarrow\operatorname{clip}\{-\theta_1/\theta_2,[-x_s,\infty)\}$; $x_s\leftarrow x_s+\alpha^*$; $e\leftarrow e-A_{*,s}\alpha^*$. Not the exact 1-D minimizer, but monotone and cheap. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Separable quadratic surrogate for the data term.</b> Write $f(x)=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|^2=f(x')+\nabla f(x')^t(x-x')+\tfrac12(x-x')^tA^tA(x-x')$ (exact Taylor) with $\nabla f(x')=A^t(Ax'-y)$. Replace $A^tA$ by any $D\succeq A^tA$ that is diagonal, e.g. $D=\lambda_{\max}(A^tA)I$ or $D_{ii}=\sum_j|[A^tA]_{ij}|$ (Gershgorin), to get a separable surrogate whose minimizer $x'-D^{-1}\nabla f(x')$ is a (scaled) gradient step. Same recipe for $\tfrac12x^tBx-b^tx$; separability decouples pixels so each update is a scalar problem and the whole image updates in parallel at $O(NM)$ instead of an $O(N^3)$ solve.`,
  R`<b>Poisson negative log-likelihood.</b> $f(\theta)=\theta-y\log\theta$ has $f'=1-y/\theta$, $f''=y/\theta^2>0$ (strictly convex on $\theta>0$), minimizer $\hat\theta=y$. On $\theta\ge\theta_{\min}$ the maximum curvature is $y/\theta_{\min}^2$, giving the surrogate $f(\theta')+f'(\theta')(\theta-\theta')+\frac{y}{2\theta_{\min}^2}(\theta-\theta')^2$ with minimizer $\theta'-f'(\theta')\theta_{\min}^2/y$. For a vector $\sum_i(x_i-y_i\log x_i)$ the surrogate is automatically separable.`,
  R`<b>EM is MM.</b> $Q(\theta;\theta')=E[\log p(y,X|\theta)\,|\,y,\theta']$ satisfies $\log p(y|\theta)\ge Q(\theta;\theta')-Q(\theta';\theta')+\log p(y|\theta')$, so it is a surrogate for <em>maximizing</em> the log-likelihood; the gradient property gives the EM convergence theorem $\nabla\log p(y|\theta^*)=0$. (Lectures 30, 32)`,
  R`<b>Sketching a surrogate.</b> Draw $f$; at $x'$ draw $q$ touching $f$ (same value, same slope) and lying above everywhere. Maximum-curvature $q$ is a parabola of curvature $c_{\max}$ centered near $x'-f'(x')/c_{\max}$; symmetric-bound $q$ is a parabola centered at $0$ touching at $\pm\Delta'$. Mark $\tilde x^*=\arg\min q$ and note $f(\tilde x^*)\le f(x')$; the tighter the surrogate, the closer $\tilde x^*$ is to $x^*$.`,
  R`<b>When surrogates fail.</b> Potentials with unbounded $\rho''(0)$ (total variation, $|\Delta|^p$ with $p&lt;2$) give infinite symmetric-bound curvature at $\Delta'=0$ and no maximum curvature; the QGGMRF and Huber, with bounded $\rho''(0)$, are the surrogate-friendly choices. Nonsmooth priors are handled instead by proximal maps and ADMM. (Lecture 20)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/gmrf"] = [
  R`<b>Causal prediction.</b> For a zero-mean Gaussian process the MMSE causal predictor $\hat X_n=E[X_n|X_i,i&lt;n]=\sum_{i&lt;n}h_{n,i}X_i$ is linear, and the errors $E_n=X_n-\hat X_n$ are independent of the past and of each other (proved with the filtration and known-quantities properties of conditional expectation). In matrix form $E=(I-H)X=AX$ with $A$ lower triangular, unit diagonal, so $\det A=1$ and $p(x)=p_E(Ax)=\dfrac{1}{(2\pi)^{N/2}|\Lambda|^{1/2}}\exp\{-\tfrac12x^tA^t\Lambda^{-1}Ax\}$, $\Lambda=\operatorname{diag}(\sigma_n^2)$, $R_X=(A^t\Lambda^{-1}A)^{-1}$. (Lecture 6)`,
  R`<b>Homogeneous AR model.</b> $E_n=X_n*(\delta_n-h_n)$ (whitening filter) and $X_n=E_n+\sum_{i=1}^Ph_iX_{n-i}$ (white-noise-driven IIR synthesis). $R_X(n)*(\delta_n-h_n)*(\delta_n-h_{-n})=\sigma_C^2\delta_n$ so $S_X(\omega)=\dfrac{\sigma_C^2}{|1-H(\omega)|^2}$. ML fit from one signal: $\hat h=\hat R^{-1}\hat b$, $\hat\sigma_C^2=\hat\sigma_x^2-\hat b^t\hat R^{-1}\hat b=\frac1N\sum_n(X_n-\hat h^tZ_n)^2$ with $Z_n$ the past-$P$ vector. (Lecture 7)`,
  R`<b>Noncausal prediction.</b> $\hat X_n=E[X_n|X_i,i\ne n]=\sum_ig_{n,i}X_i$ with $g_{n,n}=0$ (a pixel may not predict itself). The error is independent of all the predictors, but different errors are now <em>correlated</em>, so the whitening argument fails and the model is built from the precision matrix instead. $\sigma_n^2=E[E_n^2|X_{i\ne n}]$ does not depend on the conditioning values (Gaussian). (Lecture 9)`,
  R`<b>From predictor to precision matrix.</b> Differentiate $\log p(x)=\log p(x_n|x_{i\ne n})+\log p(x_{i\ne n})$ with respect to $x_n$: $\sum_iB_{n,i}x_i=\frac{1}{\sigma_n^2}\big(x_n-\sum_ig_{n,i}x_i\big)$ for all $x$, so $B_{i,j}=\frac{1}{\sigma_i^2}(\delta_{i-j}-g_{i,j})$, i.e. $B=\Gamma^{-1}(I-G)$, $\Gamma=\operatorname{diag}(\sigma_i^2)$. Inverse map: $\sigma_n^2=1/B_{n,n}$, $g_{n,i}=\delta_{n-i}-\sigma_n^2B_{n,i}=-B_{n,i}/B_{n,n}$ for $i\ne n$. (Lecture 9)`,
  R`<b>Conditional of one pixel.</b> $p(x_s|x_{r\ne s})=N\Big(-\frac{1}{B_{s,s}}\sum_{r\ne s}B_{s,r}x_r,\;\frac{1}{B_{s,s}}\Big)$: the conditional mean is the noncausal prediction, the conditional variance is the noncausal prediction variance, and it is smaller than the marginal variance $[B^{-1}]_{s,s}$. Precision large means variance small.`,
  R`<b>Zeros of $B$ and the neighborhood.</b> $B_{i,j}=0$ exactly when $X_j$ is not used to predict $X_i$. Define $\partial s=\{r\ne s:B_{s,r}\ne0\}$; symmetry of $B$ makes this a valid neighborhood system ($s\notin\partial s$, $r\in\partial s\Leftrightarrow s\in\partial r$). GMRF definition: $E[X_s|X_{r\ne s}]=E[X_s|X_{\partial s}]$. Symmetry also forces $g_{s,r}/\sigma_s^2=g_{r,s}/\sigma_r^2$, and for a homogeneous model $g_n=g_{-n}$. (Lectures 10, 11)`,
  R`<b>Homogeneous GMRF.</b> $B_{i,j}=\dfrac{\delta_{i-j}-g_{i-j}}{\sigma_{NC}^2}$, banded and Toeplitz (zero beyond $|i-j|>P$). Error covariance: from $E[E_nX_{n+k}]=\sigma_{NC}^2\delta_k$ substitute $X_{n+k}=E_{n+k}+\sum g\,X$ to get $R_E(n)=\sigma_{NC}^2(\delta_n-g_n)$, so the errors are white only if $g\equiv0$. Then $R_X*(\delta-g)*(\delta-g_-)=R_E$ gives $\sigma_{NC}^2(1-G)=S_X(1-G)^2$ and $S_X(\omega)=\dfrac{\sigma_{NC}^2}{1-G(\omega)}$, $S_E=\sigma_{NC}^2(1-G(\omega))$. Positive definiteness needs $1-G(\omega)>0$. (Lecture 10)`,
  R`<b>AR versus GMRF spectra.</b> AR: causal $h$, white errors, $S_X=\sigma_C^2/|1-H(\omega)|^2$. GMRF: symmetric $g$ ($G$ real), correlated errors, $S_X=\sigma_{NC}^2/(1-G(\omega))$. The squared magnitude versus the first power comes entirely from the whiteness of the errors. (Lecture 10)`,
  R`<b>AR to GMRF conversion.</b> Equate the two autocovariance equations: $\sigma_C^2(\delta_n-g_n)=\sigma_{NC}^2(\delta_n-h_n)*(\delta_n-h_{-n})$. At $n=0$: $\sigma_{NC}^2=\dfrac{\sigma_C^2}{1+\sum_{k=1}^Ph_k^2}$ (always $\le\sigma_C^2$), and $g_n=\delta_n-\dfrac{(\delta_n-h_n)*(\delta_n-h_{-n})}{1+\sum_kh_k^2}$: the normalized autocorrelation of the prediction-error filter. AR(1), $h_n=\rho\delta_{n-1}$: $\sigma_{NC}^2=\sigma_C^2/(1+\rho^2)$, $g_n=\frac{\rho}{1+\rho^2}(\delta_{n-1}+\delta_{n+1})$; verify both give $S_X=\frac{\sigma_C^2}{1+\rho^2}\cdot\frac{1}{1-\frac{2\rho}{1+\rho^2}\cos\omega}$. (Lecture 11)`,
  R`<b>GMRF to AR: factorization.</b> Going back requires factoring the symmetric $\delta_n-g_n$ into causal times anticausal parts (spectral factorization), always possible in 1-D: an order-$P$ AR process is exactly an order-$P$ 1-D GMRF, with $B_{i,j}=0$ for $|i-j|>P$. In 2-D the autocorrelation of a causal $P$-window is $(4P+1)\times(2P+1)$ so a 2-D AR of order $P$ is a GMRF of order $2P$, and 2-D polynomials do not factor: GMRFs are strictly more general for images. (Lecture 11)`,
  R`<b>Pairwise quadratic identity.</b> For any symmetric $B$: $x^tBx=\sum_sa_sx_s^2+\tfrac12\sum_s\sum_rb_{s,r}|x_s-x_r|^2$ with $a_s=\sum_rB_{s,r}$ (row sums) and $b_{s,r}=-B_{s,r}$ (proof: expand $\frac12\sum\sum b_{s,r}(x_s^2-2x_sx_r+x_r^2)$ and collect). Over unordered cliques the $\tfrac12$ disappears. Choosing $a_s=0$ makes the prior shift-invariant but $B$ singular (improper prior, constant images have zero energy); treat as the limit $a_s\to0$. (Lecture 15)`,
  R`<b>Boundaries and circulant models.</b> On a finite lattice $B=A^t\Lambda^{-1}A$ has Toeplitz interior rows and truncated boundary rows (only the causal terms that exist appear). With circular boundary conditions $B$ is circulant, $B_{i,j}=a_{(i-j)\bmod N}$, diagonalized by the DFT with eigenvalues $\sigma_{NC}^{-2}(1-G(2\pi k/N))$, so $|B|$ and $z=(2\pi)^{N/2}|B|^{-1/2}$ are computable. (Lecture 7)`,
  R`<b>Density and partition function.</b> $X\sim N(0,B^{-1})$: $p(x)=\frac1z\exp\{-\tfrac12x^tBx\}$ with $z=(2\pi)^{N/2}|B|^{-1/2}$. Scaling all variances by $\sigma^2$ ($B\to B/\sigma^2$) multiplies $z$ by $\sigma^N$, the special case of $z(\sigma)=\sigma^Nz_0$.`,
  R`<b>2-D GMRFs.</b> $s=(s_1,s_2)$, raster order only indexes the vector. $\partial s$ is the symmetric $(2P+1)\times(2P+1)$ window minus $s$; $E_s=X_s-\sum_{r\in\partial s}g_{s-r}X_r$, $G$ Toeplitz-block-Toeplitz, $S_X(\mu,\nu)=\dfrac{\sigma_{NC}^2}{1-G(\mu,\nu)}$ with the DSFT. A symmetric local neighborhood defines a full stationary Gaussian image model without any causal ordering artifacts. (Lecture 10)`,
  R`<b>Pitfalls.</b> Do not forget $g_{n,n}=0$; remember $B$ holds prediction coefficients scaled by $1/\sigma_n^2$ (so $g_{s,r}=-B_{s,r}/B_{s,s}$, not $-B_{s,r}$); the noncausal variance is a <em>conditional</em> variance and always $\le$ the causal one; and in a MAP problem the prior precision $B$ adds to the data precision, $R^{-1}=\frac1{\sigma^2}A^tA+B$.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/ml"] = [
  R`<b>Frequentist vocabulary.</b> $\theta$ deterministic, $\hat\theta=T(Y)$ random. $\text{bias}_\theta=E_\theta[\hat\theta]-\theta$, $\text{var}_\theta=E_\theta[(\hat\theta-\bar\theta)^2]$, $\text{MSE}_\theta=\text{var}_\theta+\text{bias}_\theta^2$; unbiased, UMVU, consistent ($\hat\theta_n\to\theta$ in probability). ML: $\hat\theta=\arg\max_\theta\log p_\theta(Y)$, necessary condition $\nabla_\theta\log p_\theta(Y)=0$. ML is generally biased but consistent and asymptotically efficient (attains the Cramér–Rao bound); use it when data far outnumber unknowns. (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>Gaussian mean.</b> i.i.d. $N(\theta,\sigma^2)$: $\log p=-\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\sum_k(Y_k-\theta)^2-\frac n2\log(2\pi\sigma^2)$; derivative $\frac{1}{\sigma^2}\sum(Y_k-\theta)=0$ gives $\hat\theta=\bar Y$, unbiased and UMVU.`,
  R`<b>Multivariate Gaussian, trace form.</b> i.i.d. $N(\mu,R)$ with $b=\sum_kx_k$, $S=\sum_kx_kx_k^t$: $\log p=-\frac n2\log|R|-\frac12\operatorname{tr}(SR^{-1})+b^tR^{-1}\mu-\frac n2\mu^tR^{-1}\mu+c$. Maximize over $\mu$ first (strictly concave quadratic): $\hat\mu=b/n$, independent of $R$. Substitute to get $g(R)=-\frac n2[\log|R|+\operatorname{tr}(\hat RR^{-1})]$ with $\hat R=S/n-\hat\mu\hat\mu^t$; with $B=\hat R^{1/2}R^{-1}\hat R^{1/2}$ (eigenvalues $\lambda_i$) this is $-\frac n2[\log|\hat R|+\sum_i(\lambda_i-\log\lambda_i)]$, minimized at all $\lambda_i=1$, so $R^*=\hat R$. Shortcut identities: $\partial\log|R|/\partial R=R^{-1}$, $\partial\operatorname{tr}(SR^{-1})/\partial R=-R^{-1}SR^{-1}$, $\operatorname{tr}(UV)=\operatorname{tr}(VU)$. (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>Bernoulli and multinomial via Lagrange multipliers.</b> Counts $N_k=\sum_n\delta(X_n-k)$: $\log p=\sum_kN_k\log\pi_k$. Maximize $\sum_kN_k\log\pi_k+\lambda(1-\sum_k\pi_k)$: $N_k/\pi_k=\lambda\Rightarrow\pi_k=N_k/\lambda$; summing to one gives $\lambda=N$ and $\hat\pi_k=N_k/N$. Bernoulli: $\hat\theta=N_1/n$. (Lectures 4, 33)`,
  R`<b>Poisson and exponential.</b> $Y\sim\mathrm{Pois}(\theta)$: $-\log p_\theta(y)=\theta-y\log\theta+\log y!$; i.i.d. NLL $N\theta-(\sum_ny_n)\log\theta+c$, derivative $N-\sum y_n/\theta=0\Rightarrow\hat\theta=\bar y$, second derivative $\sum y_n/\theta^2>0$ so strictly convex. Exponential $p(y)=\frac1\mu e^{-y/\mu}$: NLL $N\log\mu+\sum y_n/\mu$, $\hat\mu=\bar y$ (unbiased, variance $\mu^2/N$); in rate form $\lambda e^{-\lambda y}$, $\hat\lambda=1/\bar y$.`,
  R`<b>Sufficient statistics.</b> $T(Y)$ is sufficient if $p(y|\theta)=h(y)\,g(T(y),\theta)$. Then $\hat\theta_{ML}=\arg\max_\theta\log g(T(y),\theta)=f(T(y))$: the ML estimate depends on the data only through $T$. Examples: $N_1$ for Bernoulli, $(b,S)$ for Gaussians, $(\hat\sigma_x^2,\hat b,\hat R)$ for an AR model. (Lectures 3, 33)`,
  R`<b>Exponential families.</b> $p(y|\theta)=\exp\{\langle\eta(\theta),T(y)\rangle+d(\theta)+s(y)\}$; $T$ is the natural sufficient statistic and $\hat\theta_{ML}=\arg\max\{\langle\eta(\theta),T\rangle+d(\theta)\}=f(T)$ since $s(y)$ drops. i.i.d. $N(\mu,\sigma^2)$: $\eta=[\mu/\sigma^2,\,-1/(2\sigma^2)]^t$, $T=[b,S]^t$, $d=-\frac N2(\mu^2/\sigma^2+\log2\pi\sigma^2)$, giving $\hat\mu=b/N$, $\hat\sigma^2=S/N-(b/N)^2$. (Lecture 33)`,
  R`<b>More exponential families.</b> Multinomial: $\eta_k=\log\pi_k$, $T_k=N_k$. Poisson: $\eta=\log\theta$, $T=\sum y_n$, $d=-N\theta$. Exponential: $\eta=-1/\mu$, $T=\sum y_n$, $d=-N\log\mu$. Markov chain: $T=(N_j,K_{i,j})$ (initial-state and transition counts), $\hat P_{i,j}=K_{i,j}/\sum_jK_{i,j}$. Mixture complete data $(Y,X)$: $(N_m,b_m,S_m)$. (Lectures 33, 34)`,
  R`<b>ML versus Bayesian.</b> MAP $=\arg\min\{-\log p(y|x)-\log p(x)\}$ is ML with a prior added; ML is the flat-prior ($\beta\to0$) limit. Bayesian estimates trade bias for variance: for $Y=X+W$ scalar Gaussian, $\hat X_{MMSE}=\frac{\sigma_x^2}{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_w^2}Y$ attenuates toward the prior mean and $E[\hat X|X]=\frac{\sigma_x^2}{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_w^2}X\ne X$. (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>ML of an AR model.</b> $\log p(X)=-\frac N2\log(2\pi\sigma_C^2)-\frac{N}{2\sigma_C^2}(\hat\sigma_x^2-2h^t\hat b+h^t\hat Rh)$ with $\hat\sigma_x^2=\frac1N\sum X_n^2$, $\hat b=\frac1N\sum Z_nX_n$, $\hat R=\frac1N\sum Z_nZ_n^t$. Gradient in $h$: $\hat Rh=\hat b$, so $\hat h=\hat R^{-1}\hat b$; then $d/d\sigma_C^2$ gives $\hat\sigma_C^2=\hat\sigma_x^2-\hat b^t\hat R^{-1}\hat b$. One pass through the data, one linear solve. (Lecture 7)`,
  R`<b>ML of a Gibbs scale parameter.</b> For $p(x|\sigma)=\frac{1}{z(\sigma)}e^{-u(x/\sigma)}$, $z(\sigma)=\sigma^Nz_0$, so $\hat\sigma=\arg\min_\sigma\{u(x/\sigma)+N\log\sigma\}$; rooting equation $\frac{\sigma}{N}\frac{d}{d\sigma}u(x/\sigma)=-1$, no $z_0$ needed. If $u(\alpha x)=\alpha^pu(x)$ then $\hat\sigma^p=\frac pNu(x)$; for the GGMRF $\hat\sigma^p=\frac1N\sum_{\{s,r\}}b_{s,r}|x_s-x_r|^p$. (Lecture 16)`,
  R`<b>Pitfall: ad hoc estimators can be inconsistent.</b> Thresholding plant heights and averaging each group gives means that stay biased as $N\to\infty$ (inconsistent), whereas ML on the incomplete data is consistent; the difficulty is only that $p(y|\theta)=\sum_xp(y|x,\theta)p(x|\theta)$ has no closed form, which EM addresses. (Lecture 29)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/convex"] = [
  R`<b>Convexity definitions.</b> A set $\Omega$ is convex if $\lambda a+(1-\lambda)b\in\Omega$ for $a,b\in\Omega$, $\lambda\in[0,1]$. $f$ is convex if $f(\lambda a+(1-\lambda)b)\le\lambda f(a)+(1-\lambda)f(b)$; strictly convex if strict for $a\ne b$, $\lambda\in(0,1)$. Sums of convex functions are convex; convex + strictly convex is strictly convex; $f(Ax)$ is convex if $f$ is; a twice-differentiable $f$ with $\nabla^2f\succ0$ is strictly convex. (Lecture 16, Appendix A)`,
  R`<b>Local = global, and uniqueness.</b> For convex $f$ every local minimum is global (a chord from a local min to any lower point would dip below the function). For strictly convex $f$ the global minimizer is unique: if $x_1\ne x_2$ both minimize, $f(\tfrac12x_1+\tfrac12x_2)&lt;\tfrac12f(x_1)+\tfrac12f(x_2)=f^*$, a contradiction. (Lecture 17)`,
  R`<b>Compactness and existence.</b> In $\mathbb R^N$, compact = closed and bounded. A continuous function on a nonempty compact set attains its minimum (Weierstrass). Convexity alone does not give existence: $e^{-x}$ is convex with no minimizer on $\mathbb R$. (Lecture 17)`,
  R`<b>Sublevel sets are closed.</b> $A_\alpha=\{x:f(x)\le\alpha\}=f^{-1}((-\infty,\alpha])$ is the preimage of a closed set, hence closed when $f$ is continuous; convex functions on $\mathbb R^N$ are continuous. For extended functions (values $+\infty$) require lower semicontinuity, i.e. $f$ closed. (Lecture 17)`,
  R`<b>Sublevel sets are bounded (coercivity).</b> For $f(x)=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\tfrac12x^tBx$: if $A$ has full column rank with smallest singular value $\beta_{\min}>0$, then $\|y-Ax\|\ge\|Ax\|-\|y\|\ge\beta_{\min}\|x\|-\|y\|$, so $f(x)\ge\tfrac12\lambda_{\min}(\Lambda)(\beta_{\min}\|x\|-\|y\|)^2\to\infty$ as $\|x\|\to\infty$, and $A_\alpha$ is bounded. Alternatively use the prior: $\tfrac12x^tBx\ge\tfrac12\lambda_{\min}(B)\|x\|^2$ when $B\succ0$.`,
  R`<b>Existence proof template.</b> Pick $x_0$ and $\alpha=f(x_0)$; $A_\alpha$ is nonempty, closed and bounded, hence compact; $f$ attains a minimum $x^*$ on $A_\alpha$; any $x\notin A_\alpha$ has $f(x)>\alpha\ge f(x^*)$, so $x^*$ is a global minimizer. "A strictly convex function with a compact sublevel set has a unique global minimum." (Lecture 17)`,
  R`<b>Strict convexity of the MAP cost.</b> $f(x;y)=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\sum_{\{s,r\}}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)$, $b_{s,r}\ge0$, $\Lambda\succ0$, is strictly convex on convex $\Omega$ if either (1) $A$ has rank $N$ and $\rho$ is convex, or (2) $A\mathbf 1\ne0$, every pixel has a neighbor with $b_{s,r}>0$, and $\rho$ is strictly convex. Intuition for (2): the prior is flat only along constant shifts $x+c\mathbf1$, and the data term breaks that direction when $A\mathbf1\ne0$. (Lecture 17)`,
  R`<b>Stability theorem.</b> If $f(x;y)$ is continuous in $(x,y)$ and strictly convex in $x$ with a local minimum, the MAP estimate $\hat x(y)$ is unique and a continuous function of the data (Tikhonov well-posed: existence, uniqueness, stability). Nonconvex potentials (weak spring) give estimates that jump discontinuously when $y$ changes slightly. (Lectures 16, 17)`,
  R`<b>Positivity and KKT.</b> $\Omega=\mathbb R^{+N}$ is closed and convex. For convex differentiable $f$, $x^*$ is the constrained minimizer iff $\partial f(x^*)/\partial x_s=0$ where $x^*_s>0$ and $\partial f(x^*)/\partial x_s\ge0$ where $x^*_s=0$; hence $x^*$ is a global minimum iff it is a fixed point of ICD with clipped updates. (Lecture 17)`,
  R`<b>Extended convex functions.</b> Proper (never $-\infty$, not identically $+\infty$), closed (lower semicontinuous, closed epigraph / sublevel sets), convex with the convention $\infty+a=\infty$. Indicator functions of closed convex sets are proper closed convex. The proximal objective $\tfrac12\|y-x\|^2+u(x)$ of a proper closed convex $u$ is strictly convex, lower semicontinuous and coercive ($u$ is bounded below by an affine function), so its sublevel sets are compact and the proximal map exists and is unique. (Lectures 21, 23)`,
  R`<b>ICD convergence theorem.</b> If $f$ is strictly convex and continuously differentiable on convex $\Omega$, ICD converges to the global minimum. Differentiability is essential: with total variation, $\|Bx\|_1$, ICD can stall at a non-minimizer, but for $\lambda\|x\|_1$ (nonsmooth manifolds aligned with coordinate axes) it still converges. (Lectures 17, 23)`,
  R`<b>Convexity of duals.</b> The dual $g(\lambda)=\inf_xL(x;\lambda)$ is always concave (infimum of affine functions), even for nonconvex $f$; weak duality $p^*\ge d^*$ follows from the max-min inequality; Slater's condition (a strictly feasible point) plus convexity gives strong duality. (Lecture 21)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/gibbs"] = [
  R`<b>Gibbs distribution.</b> $p(x)=\frac1z\exp\{-u(x)\}$ with energy $u$ and partition function $z=\int e^{-u(x)}dx$ (a sum for discrete $x$). Any strictly positive density is Gibbs with $u=-\log p+\text{const}$. From thermodynamics $u/(k_BT)$ with $k_BT=1$; larger energy means smaller probability. (Lectures 15, 38)`,
  R`<b>Pairwise Gibbs distributions are MRFs.</b> $u(x)=\sum_{\{s,r\}\in P}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)$. Factor $p(x)=\frac1z\exp\{-\sum_{r\in\partial s}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)\}\,f(x_{r\ne s})$; dividing by $\int p\,dx_s$ cancels both $\frac1z$ and $f$, leaving $p(x_s|x_{r\ne s})=\dfrac{\exp\{-\sum_{r\in\partial s}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)\}}{\int\exp\{-\sum_{r\in\partial s}b_{s,r}\rho(x'_s-x_r)\}dx'_s}$, a function of the neighbors only. Requirements on $\rho$: $\rho(0)=0$, symmetric, monotone increasing in $|\Delta|$. (Lecture 15)`,
  R`<b>What the partition function blocks and what it does not.</b> $z$ is a sum over exponentially many states and is normally uncomputable, so ML of shape parameters ($\arg\min_\theta\{u_\theta(x)+\log z(\theta)\}$) is blocked. But $z$ does not depend on $x$: MAP needs only $\arg\min_xu(x)$ and samplers need only ratios $p(x')/p(x)=e^{u(x)-u(x')}$. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Scaling of the partition function.</b> For $p(x|\sigma)=\frac{1}{z(\sigma)}\exp\{-u(x/\sigma)\}$ on $\mathbb R^N$, substitute $x=\sigma v$, $dx=\sigma^Ndv$: $z(\sigma)=\int e^{-u(x/\sigma)}dx=\sigma^N\int e^{-u(v)}dv=\sigma^Nz_0$. Equivalently, if $X\sim p(\cdot|\sigma)$ then $X/\sigma\sim p(\cdot|1)$. (Lecture 16)`,
  R`<b>ML estimate of the scale.</b> $-\log p(x|\sigma)=u(x/\sigma)+N\log\sigma+\log z_0$; set the derivative to zero: $\frac{\sigma}{N}\frac{d}{d\sigma}u(x/\sigma)\big|_{\hat\sigma}=-1$, solvable by any 1-D rooting method. If $u(\alpha x)=\alpha^pu(x)$ then $u(x/\sigma)=\sigma^{-p}u(x)$ and $-p\sigma^{-p-1}u(x)+N/\sigma=0$ gives $\hat\sigma^p=\frac pNu(x)$; for the GGMRF $\rho=|\Delta|^p/p$ this is $\hat\sigma^p=\frac1N\sum_{\{s,r\}}b_{s,r}|x_s-x_r|^p$, the sample standard deviation when $p=2$. (Lecture 16)`,
  R`<b>Gaussian as a Gibbs distribution.</b> $u=\tfrac12x^tBx$ and $z=(2\pi)^{N/2}|B|^{-1/2}$; scaling $B\to B/\sigma^2$ multiplies $|B|^{-1/2}$ by $\sigma^N$, consistent with $z(\sigma)=\sigma^Nz_0$. The pairwise form $x^tBx=\sum_sa_sx_s^2+\sum_{\{s,r\}}b_{s,r}(x_s-x_r)^2$ shows the GMRF is the $\rho(\Delta)=\Delta^2/2$ case. (Lecture 15)`,
  R`<b>Conditional density for $Y=X+W$ (scalar).</b> $p(x|y)=\frac{1}{z_y}p(y|x)p(x)\propto\exp\{-\frac{(y-x)^2}{2\sigma_w^2}-\frac{x^2}{2\sigma_x^2}\}$. The exponent is quadratic in $x$, so the posterior is Gaussian; matching first and second derivatives gives $\sigma_{x|y}^2=\big(\frac{1}{\sigma_w^2}+\frac{1}{\sigma_x^2}\big)^{-1}=\frac{\sigma_x^2\sigma_w^2}{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_w^2}$ and $\mu_{x|y}=\frac{\sigma_x^2}{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_w^2}y$. The posterior variance does not depend on $y$; never compute $p(y)$ explicitly. (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>Vector case.</b> $X\sim N(0,R_x)$, $W\sim N(0,R_w)$ independent: $\mu_{x|y}=(R_x^{-1}+R_w^{-1})^{-1}R_w^{-1}y=R_x(R_x+R_w)^{-1}y$ and $R_{x|y}=(R_x^{-1}+R_w^{-1})^{-1}=R_x(R_x+R_w)^{-1}R_w=R_x-R_x(R_x+R_w)^{-1}R_x$; precisions add. For $Y=AX+W$ replace $R_w^{-1}$ by $A^t\Lambda A$ and $R_w^{-1}y$ by $A^t\Lambda y$. (Lectures 4, 12)`,
  R`<b>MMSE versus MAP.</b> Squared-error Bayes risk is minimized by $E[X|Y]$ (expand $E[(X-\hat x)^2|Y]$ and differentiate); zero-one cost gives the posterior mode; absolute error the median. For jointly Gaussian $(X,Y)$ the posterior is symmetric and unimodal so all three coincide and the estimator is linear in $y$. (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>Non-Gaussian priors.</b> With Laplacian, generalized-Gaussian or mixture priors the posterior is asymmetric or multimodal, so MMSE $\ne$ MAP: MAP is what optimization computes, MMSE is what an MSE-trained denoiser approximates (Lecture 26 footnote). Tweedie's formula: $E[X|\tilde X]=\tilde X+\sigma^2\nabla\log p_\sigma(\tilde X)$ for $\tilde X=X+\sigma W$, so the MMSE denoiser is a step along the score of the <em>noisy</em> density. (GPnP notes)`,
  R`<b>Discrete Gibbs models.</b> Ising/Potts: $u(x)=\beta\sum_{\{r,s\}}\delta(x_r\ne x_s)=\beta\times$(boundary length); $\beta>0$ favors smooth blobs, $\beta&lt;0$ checkerboards, $\beta=0$ independence. Conditional of a pixel depends on the number of disagreeing neighbors only. Hammersley–Clifford: with $p>0$, MRF $\Leftrightarrow$ Gibbs. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>MAP with a Gibbs prior.</b> $\hat x=\arg\min_x\{\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+u(x)\}$; $\log z$ is a constant. Estimating $\sigma$ (or $\beta$) jointly with $x$ is a model-selection problem: too large $\sigma_x$ under-regularizes (noisy), too small over-regularizes (over-smoothed). (Lecture 16)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/midterm/words"] = [
  R`<b>How to answer.</b> Name each object in the equation, say what each term "wants", describe the tension or balance between them, then say what happens in the limits (parameter to 0 or infinity, noise-free, no prior). Fifty accurate words with no symbols beat a page of algebra; the notes call this "emotional intuition", and it is graded on whether the interpretation is right.`,
  R`<b>MAP estimate</b> $\hat x=\arg\min_x\{-\log p(y|x)-\log p(x)\}$: "the image that best explains the measurements while remaining a plausible image. The first term is the physics of the sensor, the second is everything we believe about images before looking; the minimum is a negotiated compromise between the two." (Lecture 12)`,
  R`<b>MMSE attenuation</b> $\hat X=\frac{\sigma_x^2}{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_w^2}Y$: "pull the noisy measurement toward the prior mean; the noisier the data relative to the signal, the harder the pull. We accept bias to buy a large reduction in variance." (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>Precision matrix</b> $B=\Gamma^{-1}(I-G)$: "$B$ describes how every pixel is predicted from the others: its diagonal is one over the prediction variance, its off-diagonals are the (scaled, negated) prediction weights, and its zeros say which pixels ignore each other. Covariance describes how pairs vary together; precision describes who predicts whom." (Lecture 9)`,
  R`<b>GMRF versus AR spectrum</b> $S_X=\sigma_{NC}^2/(1-G)$ vs $\sigma_C^2/|1-H|^2$: "the AR errors are white so the filter appears squared; the noncausal errors are correlated with exactly the prediction filter, and one factor cancels." (Lecture 10)`,
  R`<b>Gradient descent step size</b> $\tilde\epsilon_i^{(k)}=(1-\alpha h_i)^k\tilde\epsilon_i^{(0)}$: "one step size must serve every direction; the steep direction (largest eigenvalue) limits how big it can be, the flat direction (smallest) then crawls. For deblurring the flat directions are the high frequencies, so edges appear last." (Lecture 13)`,
  R`<b>ICD update</b> $x_s\leftarrow\frac{y_s+\lambda\sum g_{s-r}x_r}{1+\lambda}$: "each pixel is replaced by a weighted average of what the sensor says and what its neighbors predict, weighted by how much we trust each; clean data trusts the measurement, noisy data trusts the neighbors." (Lecture 14)`,
  R`<b>Potential and influence functions</b> $\rho$, $\rho'$: "the potential is the energy stored between two neighboring pixels, the influence function is the force one exerts on the other. A quadratic pulls harder the farther apart they are, so it drags pixels across an edge together; an edge-preserving potential lets the force level off or fall to zero so an edge can survive. Convex potentials give a stable answer; nonconvex ones give sharper edges but an estimate that can jump." (Lecture 16)`,
  R`<b>Surrogate function</b> $f(x)\le Q(x;x')-Q(x';x')+f(x')$: "an easier stand-in that agrees with the true cost where we stand and lies above it everywhere else; lowering the stand-in is guaranteed to lower the truth, so we may repeatedly minimize the easy function instead of the hard one." (Lecture 19)`,
  R`<b>Proximal map</b> $H(y)=\arg\min_x\{\tfrac12\|y-x\|^2+u(x)\}$: "a cart on the hill $u$ tied by a spring to a stake at $y$; it rolls toward the valley but the spring holds it back, and it rests where the two forces balance. It turns an objective into an action: put in a point, get out a better point." (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Shrinkage</b> $S_\lambda(y)=\operatorname{sign}(y)\max\{|y|-\lambda,0\}$: "values smaller than the threshold are probably noise, so kill them; larger values are probably signal, so keep them but pull them toward zero by the same amount. It is a soft threshold: continuous, so no value is treated very differently from its neighbor." (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Augmented Lagrangian multiplier</b> $u\leftarrow u+(\hat x-\hat v)$: "$u$ is a running memory of how much the constraint has been violated; each iteration it leans harder in the direction of past violations until the two split copies agree exactly, which a fixed penalty alone can never force." (Lectures 21, 22)`,
  R`<b>PnP / consensus equilibrium</b> $x^*=F(x^*-u^*)=H(x^*+u^*)$: "two agents, one loyal to the sensor and one loyal to what images look like, are each handed the consensus image shifted by an equal and opposite offset and each hands back the same image; that agreement, not the minimum of any cost, is the answer." (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Detailed balance</b> $\pi_iP_{i,j}=\pi_jP_{j,i}$: "in steady state the probability flowing from $i$ to $j$ equals the flow from $j$ back to $i$, for every pair; a movie of the chain looks the same played backwards. Whenever you can solve these you have the stationary distribution for free." (Lecture 37)`,
  R`<b>Mann iteration</b> $w\leftarrow(1-\rho)w+\rho Tw$: "take only a fraction of the step the operator suggests. A pure rotation never settles, but averaging with where you were damps the spin and drifts you to the fixed point; that is what lets plug-in denoisers converge." (Lecture 27)`
];

/* ===================== FINAL ===================== */

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/em"] = [
  R`<b>Vocabulary and goal.</b> Missing data $X$ (class labels), complete data $(X,Y)$, incomplete data $Y$. Goal: ML estimate $\hat\theta=\arg\max_\theta\log p(y|\theta)$ from $Y$ alone, where $p(y|\theta)=\sum_xp(y|x,\theta)p(x|\theta)$ is a product of sums with no closed form. The threshold-and-average heuristic is inconsistent (biased even as $N\to\infty$); EM formalizes "guess the labels, re-estimate, repeat" so that it provably works. (Lecture 29)`,
  R`<b>Mixture template.</b> $X_n$ i.i.d. with $P\{X_n=m\}=\pi_m$; given $X_n=m$, $Y_n\sim p_m(y_n|\theta_m)$ independent. Joint (complete-data) density $p(y,x|\theta)=\prod_n\pi_{x_n}p_{x_n}(y_n)$, whose log is $\sum_n\sum_m\delta(x_n-m)\{\log p_m(y_n)+\log\pi_m\}$. Incomplete density $p(y|\theta)=\prod_n\sum_m\pi_mp_m(y_n)$. Write these first on any EM problem. (Lectures 29, 30)`,
  R`<b>Decomposition of the log-likelihood.</b> $\log p(y|\theta)=Q(\theta;\theta')+H(\theta;\theta')$ with $Q(\theta;\theta')=E[\log p(y,X|\theta)\,|\,Y=y,\theta']$ and $H(\theta;\theta')=-E[\log p(X|y,\theta)\,|\,Y=y,\theta']$. Proof: $\log p(y|\theta)=\log\frac{p(y,X|\theta)}{p(X|y,\theta)}$ for every $X$; take $E[\cdot|Y=y,\theta']$ (the left side is not random) and split the log of the ratio. Note the densities carry $\theta$, the expectation carries $\theta'$. (Lecture 30)`,
  R`<b>$H$ is minimized at $\theta=\theta'$ (Jensen).</b> $0=\log\int\frac{p(x|y,\theta)}{p(x|y,\theta')}p(x|y,\theta')dx\ge\int\log\frac{p(x|y,\theta)}{p(x|y,\theta')}p(x|y,\theta')dx=-H(\theta;\theta')+H(\theta';\theta')$, using concavity of $\log$. So $H(\theta;\theta')\ge H(\theta';\theta')$ (this is just KL divergence $\ge0$). (Lecture 30)`,
  R`<b>Key bound and surrogate property.</b> Subtracting the decompositions at $\theta$ and $\theta'$ and dropping $H(\theta;\theta')-H(\theta';\theta')\ge0$: $\log p(y|\theta)-\log p(y|\theta')\ge Q(\theta;\theta')-Q(\theta';\theta')$. Hence $Q$ is a surrogate for maximizing the log-likelihood and $Q(\theta;\theta')>Q(\theta';\theta')\Rightarrow p(y|\theta)>p(y|\theta')$. Sketch: $\log p(y|\theta)$ above, $Q(\theta;\theta')$ shifted up by $H(\theta';\theta')$ touches it at $\theta'$ and lies below elsewhere. (Lecture 30)`,
  R`<b>The EM iteration.</b> E-step: $Q(\theta;\theta^{(k)})=E[\log p(y,X|\theta)\,|\,Y=y,\theta^{(k)}]$. M-step: $\theta^{(k+1)}=\arg\max_\theta Q(\theta;\theta^{(k)})$. Then $\log p(y|\theta^{(k+1)})\ge\log p(y|\theta^{(k)})$; if the ML estimate exists the bounded monotone sequence converges to some $L^*$. Convergence theorem (Lecture 32): if $\Omega$ is open, $Q,H$ are $C^1$ and $\theta^{(k)}\to\theta^*$, then $\nabla\log p(y|\theta^*)=\nabla Q(\theta^*;\theta^*)=0$ (gradient property of surrogates). Local maxima are still possible.`,
  R`<b>Q for a Gaussian mixture.</b> Move the expectation inside the double sum; $E[\delta(X_n-m)|y,\theta']=P\{X_n=m|y,\theta'\}$, so $Q(\theta;\theta')=\sum_n\sum_mP\{X_n=m|y,\theta'\}\big\{-\frac{(y_n-\mu_m)^2}{2\sigma_m^2}-\tfrac12\log(2\pi\sigma_m^2)+\log\pi_m\big\}$: a posterior-weighted complete-data log-likelihood. The braces depend on the new $\theta$, the weights on the old $\theta'$. (Lecture 30)`,
  R`<b>E-step = soft classification (Bayes' rule).</b> $f(m|y_n,\theta')=P_{n,m}=\dfrac{p_m(y_n|\theta')\,\pi'_m}{\sum_jp_j(y_n|\theta')\,\pi'_j}$. Each sample gets partial membership in every class; EM generalizes the hard threshold heuristic by replacing hard labels with these probabilities. (Lecture 32)`,
  R`<b>M-step for Gaussian mixtures.</b> Maximize $Q$ exactly as in ML with weights: $\bar N_m\leftarrow\sum_nP_{n,m}$, $\pi_m\leftarrow\bar N_m/N$ (Lagrange multiplier for $\sum\pi_m=1$), $\mu_m\leftarrow\frac{1}{\bar N_m}\sum_ny_nP_{n,m}$, $\sigma_m^2\leftarrow\frac{1}{\bar N_m}\sum_n(y_n-\mu_m)^2P_{n,m}=\bar S_m/\bar N_m-\mu_m^2$. Pseudo-code: assign $P_{n,m}$ for all $n,m$; then assign $N_m,\pi_m,\mu_m,\sigma_m^2$ for all $m$; repeat. (Lecture 32)`,
  R`<b>Exponential-family shortcut.</b> If $p(y,x|\theta)=\exp\{\langle g(\theta),T(y,x)\rangle+d(\theta)+s(y,x)\}$ then $Q(\theta;\theta')=\langle g(\theta),\bar T(y)\rangle+d(\theta)+\text{const}$ with $\bar T(y)=E[T(y,X)\,|\,Y=y,\theta']$. So the EM update is the ML formula $\theta_{ML}=f(T)$ evaluated at the expected statistic: $\theta''=f(\bar T)$. No $Q$-function calculation is needed. (Lecture 33)`,
  R`<b>Natural sufficient statistics for mixtures.</b> $N_m=\sum_n\delta(x_n-m)$, $b_m=\sum_ny_n\delta(x_n-m)$, $S_m=\sum_ny_ny_n^t\delta(x_n-m)$ (scalar case $S_m=\sum y_n^2\delta$). Complete-data ML: $\hat\pi_m=N_m/N$, $\hat\mu_m=b_m/N_m$, $\hat R_m=S_m/N_m-b_mb_m^t/N_m^2$. E-step: $\bar N_m=\sum_nP_{n,m}$, $\bar b_m=\sum_ny_nP_{n,m}$, $\bar S_m=\sum_ny_ny_n^tP_{n,m}$; M-step: same formulas with bars. (Lecture 33)`,
  R`<b>Poisson, exponential and discrete mixtures.</b> Poisson $p_m(y)=\lambda_m^ye^{-\lambda_m}/y!$: complete-data NLL $\sum_m\{N_m\lambda_m-b_m\log\lambda_m\}+\dots$, so $\hat\lambda_m=b_m/N_m$ and EM sets $\lambda_m\leftarrow\bar b_m/\bar N_m$ with $P_{n,m}\propto\pi_m\lambda_m^{y_n}e^{-\lambda_m}$. Exponential $p_m(y)=\frac{1}{\mu_m}e^{-y/\mu_m}$: $\mu_m\leftarrow\bar b_m/\bar N_m$. Discrete $P\{Y_n=k|X_n=m\}=\theta_{m,k}$: statistics $N_{m,k}=\sum_n\delta(x_n-m)\delta(y_n-k)$, update $\theta_{m,k}\leftarrow\bar N_{m,k}/\bar N_m$ with $\bar N_{m,k}=\sum_{n:y_n=k}P_{n,m}$.`,
  R`<b>Deriving the complete-data ML.</b> Write $\log p(y,x|\theta)$ as a sum over classes; the $\pi$ part $\sum_mN_m\log\pi_m$ is maximized with a Lagrange multiplier ($\hat\pi_m=N_m/N$); each class parameter appears only in its own term, so differentiate class by class (e.g. $\frac{d}{d\lambda_m}[N_m\lambda_m-b_m\log\lambda_m]=0$). Always check the second derivative or convexity so the stationary point is a maximum.`,
  R`<b>EM as MM.</b> $Q$ is a surrogate for maximization, so the five surrogate properties apply: monotonicity (likelihood never decreases), gradient equality at $\theta'$, fixed point $\Rightarrow$ stationary point, additivity over independent samples, composition. "Sketch $Q$" questions: draw $\log p(y|\theta)$, draw $Q(\theta;\theta')+H(\theta';\theta')$ tangent at $\theta'$ and below, mark $\theta''=\arg\max Q$ and note the likelihood at $\theta''$ is at least as large. (Lectures 19, 32)`,
  R`<b>Pitfalls.</b> Gaussian mixtures with unconstrained variances have unbounded likelihood ($\sigma_m^2\to0$ on one sample); initialization matters (local maxima); the guarantee is on $L(\theta^{(k)})$, not on $\theta^{(k)}$; the E-step uses the <em>old</em> parameters everywhere, the M-step the expected statistics; $\bar N_m$ can be fractional. Order selection (how many classes) uses MDL, not ML.`,
  R`<b>Beyond mixtures.</b> The same machinery handles Markov chains and HMMs (expected transition counts $\bar K_{i,j}$, then $P_{i,j}\leftarrow\bar K_{i,j}/\sum_j\bar K_{i,j}$) and, in GPnP-EM, forward-model parameters $\phi$ where the E-step is replaced by posterior samples: $Q_n(\phi)=\frac1K\sum_k\tfrac12\|y-A_\phi X_k\|^2$, M-step $\phi\leftarrow\arg\min_\phi Q_n(\phi)+s(\phi)$. (Lecture 34, GPnP notes)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/mc"] = [
  R`<b>Definition and notation.</b> $\{X_n\}$ on countable $\Omega=\{0,\dots,M-1\}$ is a Markov chain if $P\{X_n=j|X_k,k&lt;n\}=P\{X_n=j|X_{n-1}\}$. Marginal $\pi_j^{(n)}=P\{X_n=j\}$, transitions $P_{i,j}=P\{X_n=j|X_{n-1}=i\}$ (rows sum to 1), initial $\tau_j=\pi_j^{(0)}$. Homogeneous: $P$ does not depend on $n$; homogeneous does <em>not</em> mean stationary (transients depend on $\tau$). Sequence probability $p(x)=\tau_{x_0}\prod_{n=1}^NP_{x_{n-1},x_n}$. (Lecture 34)`,
  R`<b>ML estimation of a chain.</b> Statistics $N_j=\delta(X_0-j)$ and $K_{i,j}=\sum_n\delta(X_n-j)\delta(X_{n-1}-i)$ (transition counts); $\log p(x)=\sum_j\{N_j\log\tau_j+\sum_iK_{i,j}\log P_{i,j}\}$ is an exponential family, so $\hat\tau_j=N_j$ and $\hat P_{i,j}=K_{i,j}/\sum_jK_{i,j}$: count transitions, normalize each row. Binary flip chain: $p(x)=\tfrac12(1-\rho)^{N-K}\rho^K$ with $K$ = number of state changes, $\hat\rho=K/N$. (Lecture 34)`,
  R`<b>Evolution of the marginal.</b> $\pi_j^{(n+1)}=\sum_i\pi_i^{(n)}P_{i,j}$, i.e. the row vector $\pi^{(n+1)}=\pi^{(n)}P$ and $\pi^{(n)}=\pi^{(0)}P^n$. If a steady state independent of $\pi^{(0)}$ exists then $P^\infty=\mathbf 1\pi^\infty$ (every row equals $\pi^\infty$) and $\pi^\infty=\pi^\infty P$: $\pi^\infty$ is a left eigenvector of $P$ with eigenvalue 1. (Lecture 36)`,
  R`<b>Three ways to fail.</b> Trapped in disconnected states ($X_{n+1}=X_n$: limit depends on $\pi^{(0)}$); periodic ($X_{n+1}=-X_n$: $P\{X_n=1\}$ alternates, no limit); dispersed (symmetric random walk on $\mathbb Z$: $\pi_i^{(n)}\to0$ for every $i$). Irreducibility, aperiodicity and finiteness rule these out respectively. (Lecture 36)`,
  R`<b>Irreducible.</b> States $i,j$ communicate if $[P^n]_{i,j}>0$ and $[P^m]_{j,i}>0$ for some $n,m>0$ (an equivalence relation). The chain is irreducible if all pairs communicate. To prove it, exhibit a positive-probability path from every state to every other (draw the state graph); to disprove it, show a state or set with no return path. (Lecture 36)`,
  R`<b>Periodic and aperiodic.</b> The period of $i$ is $d(i)=\gcd\{n:[P^n]_{i,i}>0\}$ (the largest $d$ with $[P^n]_{i,i}=0$ unless $d\mid n$); periodic if $d(i)>1$. Communicating states share the same period, so an irreducible chain is aperiodic if one state has $d=1$; a self-loop $P_{i,i}>0$ suffices. The deterministic cycle $P_{i,(i+1)\bmod M}=1$ is irreducible with period $M$: the uniform $\pi$ solves the balance equations but $P^n$ does not converge. (Lecture 36)`,
  R`<b>Limit theorem / ergodicity.</b> Finite $\Omega$ + irreducible + aperiodic $\Rightarrow$ a unique stationary distribution with $\pi_j=\lim_n[P^n]_{i,j}>0$ for every start $i$, the unique solution of the full-balance equations (FBE) $\pi_j=\sum_i\pi_iP_{i,j}$, $\sum_i\pi_i=1$. Such chains are ergodic: time averages equal expectations under $\pi$, $\frac1N\sum_ng(X_n)\to E_\pi[g]$. (Lecture 36)`,
  R`<b>Structural properties.</b> Conditioning on the past depends only on the latest sample: $P\{X_n|X_r,r\le k\}=P\{X_n|X_k\}$. Subsampling $Y_n=X_{dn}$ is again a Markov chain (homogeneous if $X$ is). Time reversal $Y_n=X_{N-n}$ is a Markov chain (Bayes' rule reverses conditioning); in steady state its transitions are $Q_{j,i}=P\{X_{n-1}=i|X_n=j\}=\pi_iP_{i,j}/\pi_j$, so $\pi_iP_{i,j}=\pi_jQ_{j,i}$ always. (Lecture 37)`,
  R`<b>Reversibility and detailed balance.</b> The chain is reversible under $\pi$ if $Q=P$, i.e. the detailed-balance equations (DBE) $\pi_iP_{i,j}=\pi_jP_{j,i}$ hold for all $i,j$ with $\sum\pi_i=1$: the rate of $i\to j$ transitions equals the rate of $j\to i$. DBE $\Rightarrow$ FBE: sum over $i$, $\sum_i\pi_iP_{i,j}=\pi_j\sum_iP_{j,i}=\pi_j$. Not every chain is reversible (a one-way 3-cycle with uniform $\pi$ violates DBE), but when DBE has a solution it is <em>the</em> stationary distribution. (Lecture 37)`,
  R`<b>Birth–death chains.</b> $P_{i,i+1}=\lambda$, $P_{i,i-1}=\mu$ ($i>0$), stay otherwise. Nearest-neighbor structure makes DBE solvable: $\pi_i\lambda=\pi_{i+1}\mu\Rightarrow\pi_i=\frac1z(\lambda/\mu)^i$. If $\lambda/\mu&lt;1$, $z=1/(1-\lambda/\mu)$ and $\pi_i=(1-\lambda/\mu)(\lambda/\mu)^i$ (ergodic, reversible); if $\lambda\ge\mu$ the only solution is $\pi\equiv0$: no steady state, the state drifts to infinity. State-dependent rates: $\pi_{i+1}=\pi_i\lambda_i/\mu_{i+1}$, then normalize. (Lecture 37)`,
  R`<b>Doubly stochastic and symmetric $P$.</b> If every column also sums to one, the uniform $\pi_i=1/M$ satisfies FBE ($\sum_i\frac1MP_{i,j}=\frac1M$). If $P=P^t$ the uniform distribution also satisfies DBE, so the chain is reversible and $Q=P^t=P$. For uniform $\pi$ in general, $Q=P^t$.`,
  R`<b>Exam recipe.</b> (1) Write $P$ from the description and draw the graph. (2) Irreducible: paths both ways. (3) Aperiodic: self-loop or gcd argument. (4) Stationary $\pi$: try DBE first (birth–death, symmetric), else solve FBE $\pi=\pi P$ with $\sum\pi_i=1$ (one equation is redundant). (5) Reversed chain $Q_{j,i}=\pi_iP_{i,j}/\pi_j$; reversible iff $Q=P$. (6) $\lim_nP^n=\mathbf1\pi$ only if ergodic; a periodic chain has a stationary $\pi$ but no limit. (7) Ergodic means time averages converge to $\pi$-expectations.`,
  R`<b>Chains as MRFs and as a computational tool.</b> A Markov chain is a 1-D Gibbs distribution with cliques $\{n-1,n\}$ and $V=-\log p(x_n|x_{n-1})$; conversely a 1-D MRF of order $P$ is a Markov chain of order $P$. Ergodic theory is what makes MCMC work: samplers are designed so that the target Gibbs distribution satisfies DBE. (Lectures 37, 38, 39)`,
  R`<b>Continuous state.</b> With $\Omega$ continuous the object is a discrete-time Markov process; all definitions carry over with sums replaced by integrals ($\pi(x')=\int\pi(x)p(x'|x)dx$, DBE $\pi(x)p(x'|x)=\pi(x')p(x|x')$), which is the setting of Metropolis sampling of continuous MRFs.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/pnp"] = [
  R`<b>Constrained problem, Lagrangian, duality.</b> $p^*=\inf_{x\in\Omega,\,Cx=b}f(x)$; Lagrangian $L(x;\lambda)=f(x)+\lambda^t(Cx-b)$; dual $g(\lambda)=\inf_{x\in\Omega}L(x;\lambda)$ is always concave (infimum of affine functions). Weak duality $p^*\ge d^*=\sup_\lambda g(\lambda)$ from the max-min inequality; strong duality $p^*=d^*$ holds under Slater's condition (a strictly feasible interior point) for convex $f$, and then $x^*$ minimizes $L(x;\lambda^*)$ over $\Omega$. Catch: $g$ has no explicit form, so $\lambda^*$ cannot be found by maximizing it directly. (Lecture 21)`,
  R`<b>Augmented Lagrangian.</b> $L(x;a,\lambda)=f(x)+\lambda^t(Cx-b)+\frac a2\|Cx-b\|^2$. Lemma: $\inf_xL(x;a,\lambda)=\sup_{\lambda'}\{g(\lambda')-\frac{1}{2a}\|\lambda'-\lambda\|^2\}$ with $\lambda^{**}=\lambda+a(Cx^{**}-b)$. Algorithm: $x^{(k+1)}=\arg\min_{x\in\Omega}L(x;a,\lambda^{(k)})$, $\lambda^{(k+1)}=\lambda^{(k)}+a(Cx^{(k+1)}-b)$; in dual space this is a proximal step on $-g$, so $\lambda^{(k)}\to\lambda^*$ for any $a>0$. Scaled form with $u=\lambda/a$: minimize $f(x)+\frac a2\|Cx-b+u\|^2$, then $u\leftarrow u+Cx-b$. Large $a$: fewer iterations but stiffer $x$-updates. (Lecture 22)`,
  R`<b>Positivity by splitting.</b> Write the constraint as $\tilde g(x)=0$ on $\mathbb R^{+N}$, $\infty$ elsewhere (proper, closed, convex); split $x=v$ and use the augmented Lagrangian $f(x)+\tilde g(v)+\frac a2\|x-v+u\|^2$. ADMM for positivity: $\hat x\leftarrow\arg\min_x\{f(x)+\frac a2\|x-(\hat v-u)\|^2\}$ (unconstrained), $\hat v\leftarrow\operatorname{clip}\{\hat x+u,\mathbb R^{+N}\}$, $u\leftarrow u+\hat x-\hat v$. (Lecture 21)`,
  R`<b>General ADMM.</b> For $\min_{x\in\Omega}f(x)+g(Bx)$: split $Bx=v$, then repeat $\hat x\leftarrow\arg\min_{x\in\Omega}\{f(x)+\frac a2\|Bx-(\hat v-u)\|^2\}$, $\hat v\leftarrow\arg\min_v\{g(v)+\frac a2\|v-(B\hat x+u)\|^2\}$, $u\leftarrow u+B\hat x-\hat v$. Three tricks: split, augmented Lagrangian, a single alternating pass instead of nested inner iterations. Only the $x$-update carries the constraint $x\in\Omega$. Convergence (saddle point of the unaugmented Lagrangian exists by strong duality): residual $\to0$, cost $\to p^*$, $u\to\lambda^*/a$. (Lectures 24, 25)`,
  R`<b>Proximal maps as MAP estimates.</b> $F(z)=\arg\min_x\{f(x)+\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\|x-z\|^2\}$: with $f=-\log p(y|x)$ this is the MAP estimate of $x$ from $y$ under the prior $X\sim N(z,\sigma^2I)$. $H(z)=\arg\min_x\{\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\|z-x\|^2+h(x)\}$: MAP denoiser for the virtual model $Z=X+W$, $W\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, prior $p(x)\propto e^{-h(x)}$. Given $H$, "state the implied forward and prior models": AWGN of variance $\sigma^2$ and $e^{-h}$. (Lecture 26)`,
  R`<b>Explicit $F$ for common forward models.</b> Gaussian $f(x)=\frac{1}{2\sigma_y^2}\|y-Ax\|^2$, $a=1/\sigma^2$: $F(z)=(A^tA/\sigma_y^2+aI)^{-1}(A^ty/\sigma_y^2+az)$. Poisson $Y_i\sim\mathrm{Pois}(x_i)$: $f(x)=\sum_i(x_i-y_i\log x_i)$ is separable; per pixel $1-y_i/x_i+(x_i-z_i)/\sigma^2=0$, i.e. $x_i^2+(\sigma^2-z_i)x_i-\sigma^2y_i=0$, so $x_i=\tfrac12\big[(z_i-\sigma^2)+\sqrt{(z_i-\sigma^2)^2+4\sigma^2y_i}\big]$ (positive root). Indicator of a convex set: $F=\operatorname{clip}$. $\lambda\|x\|_1$: $H(z)=S_{\lambda\sigma^2}(z)$. (Lectures 23, 25)`,
  R`<b>PnP-ADMM and the plug-in denoiser.</b> $x\leftarrow F(v-u)$, $v\leftarrow H(x+u)$, $u\leftarrow u+(x-v)$. Replace $H$ by a denoiser $\hat H$ trained on pairs $(V_n,X_n=V_n+\sigma W_n)$ with loss $\sum_n\|V_n-H_\theta(X_n)\|^2$; this approximates the MMSE denoiser $E[V|X]$ rather than the MAP denoiser, and the algorithm is now more general than MAP because $\hat H$ need not be a proximal map. Regularization strength is set by the training noise level $\sigma$. (Lecture 26)`,
  R`<b>Moreau's theorem.</b> $H$ is the proximal map of a proper closed convex function iff it is nonexpansive and conservative ($H=\nabla\phi$), which requires a self-adjoint Jacobian $\nabla H=[\nabla H]^t$ ("zero curl"). Linear $H(x)=Ax$ needs $A$ symmetric with eigenvalues in $[0,1]$. A trained CNN essentially never satisfies this, so PnP with a CNN is not minimizing any cost. (Lecture 26)`,
  R`<b>Consensus equilibrium equations.</b> At a fixed point of PnP-ADMM, $x=v$, so $x^*=F(x^*-u^*)$ and $x^*=H(x^*+u^*)$. Proof that the equilibrium implies $\nabla f+\nabla h=0$: optimality of $F$ gives $\nabla f(x^*)+\frac{1}{\sigma^2}(x^*-(x^*-u^*))=\nabla f(x^*)+u^*/\sigma^2=0$; optimality of $H$ gives $\nabla h(x^*)-u^*/\sigma^2=0$; add. Equivalence theorem: for proximal maps of proper closed convex $f,h$, $x^*$ solves CE iff it is a global minimizer of $f+h$ (converse: take $u^*\in-\sigma^2\nabla f(x^*)$). For general agents CE is a force balance that minimizes nothing. (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Fixed point of $T=(2H-I)(2F-I)$.</b> With $w_1=x-u$, $w_2=x+u$: $F(w_1^*)=x^*=\tfrac12(w_1^*+w_2^*)$, so $2F(w_1^*)-w_1^*=w_2^*$, i.e. $(2F-I)w_1^*=w_2^*$; likewise $(2H-I)w_2^*=w_1^*$; compose to get $Tw_1^*=w_1^*$ with $w_1^*=x^*-u^*$. Conversely from a fixed point recover $w_2^*=(2F-I)w_1^*$, $x^*=\tfrac12(w_1^*+w_2^*)$, $u^*=\tfrac12(w_2^*-w_1^*)$. (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Nonexpansive, firmly nonexpansive, contraction.</b> $T$ nonexpansive: $\|Tx-Ty\|\le\|x-y\|$; contraction: $\le L\|x-y\|$, $L&lt;1$ (Banach: unique fixed point, $x\leftarrow Tx$ converges geometrically). Firmly nonexpansive: $\|Tx-Ty\|^2\le\langle Tx-Ty,x-y\rangle$; proximal maps are firmly nonexpansive (from monotonicity of the subgradient: $\langle Fx-Fy,(x-Fx)-(y-Fy)\rangle\ge0$). Proof that $2F-I$ is nonexpansive: $\|(2F-I)x-(2F-I)y\|^2=4\|Fx-Fy\|^2-4\langle Fx-Fy,x-y\rangle+\|x-y\|^2\le\|x-y\|^2$. A composition of nonexpansive maps is nonexpansive, so $T$ is nonexpansive whenever $F$ and $\hat H$ are firmly nonexpansive. (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Mann iteration and PnP-DR.</b> Plain $w\leftarrow Tw$ can fail for nonexpansive $T$ (a rotation cycles forever), but $w\leftarrow(1-\rho)w+\rho Tw$, $\rho\in(0,1)$, converges to a fixed point of $T$ if one exists (for a rotation the averaged map has eigenvalues $(1-\rho)+\rho e^{i\theta}$ of modulus below one). PnP-DR-serial: repeat $w'\leftarrow(2H-I)(2F-I)w$, $w\leftarrow(1-\rho)w+\rho w'$; then $x^*=F(w)$. With $\rho=\tfrac12$ this is exactly PnP-ADMM, which finally proves ADMM converges; larger $\rho$ often converges faster. (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Parallel form and MACE.</b> Stack $w=[w_1;w_2]$, $\mathbf F(w)=[F(w_1);H(w_2)]$, $G(w)=[\bar w;\bar w]$ with $\bar w=\tfrac12(w_1+w_2)$. CE reads $\mathbf F(w^*)=G(w^*)$; since $GG=G$, $(2G-I)^2=I$ and $w^*$ is a fixed point of $T=(2G-I)(2\mathbf F-I)$. MACE generalizes to $K$ agents with weights $\mu_k$ ($\sum\mu_k=1$, $\bar w=\sum\mu_kw_k$): with proximal agents the solution minimizes $\sum_k\mu_kf_k$; with general agents it balances their actions; the weights adjust regularization without retraining. MACE-DR: $x\leftarrow\mathbf F(w)$, $z\leftarrow G_\mu(2x-w)$, $w\leftarrow w+2\rho(z-x)$. Wrinkle: for unequal $\mu_k$, $G_\mu$ may be expansive and convergence is not guaranteed. (Lectures 27, 28)`,
  R`<b>Shrinkage and total variation.</b> $\operatorname{prox}_{\lambda\|\cdot\|_1}(y)=S_\lambda(y)=\operatorname{sign}(y)\max\{|y|-\lambda,0\}$ componentwise (derive by the three cases). TV regularization $\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\|Bx\|_1$: ICD and surrogate methods fail (nondifferentiable, unbounded $\rho''(0)$), but ADMM with $v=Bx$ gives a quadratic $x$-update and $\hat v\leftarrow S_{1/a}(B\hat x+u)$. (Lectures 23, 25)`,
  R`<b>MMSE versus MAP in PnP.</b> For Gaussian $X$ the MMSE and MAP denoisers coincide (linear Wiener filter $R_x(R_x+\sigma^2I)^{-1}z$); in general the MMSE denoiser is the conditional mean and by Tweedie's formula $E[X|\tilde X]=\tilde X+\sigma^2\nabla\log p_\sigma(\tilde X)$, so an MSE-trained denoiser learns the score of the noisy prior. GPnP uses proximal <em>generators</em> (sample from $\propto\exp\{-u_k(x)-\frac{1}{2\gamma^2}\|x-v\|^2\}$) alternating between prior and forward model to sample the posterior instead of finding its mode. (GPnP notes)`,
  R`<b>Interpretation of $u^*$.</b> $u^*=-\sigma^2\nabla f(x^*)=\sigma^2\nabla h(x^*)$ is the offset each agent needs: $H$ is fed $x^*+u^*$ (the consensus image plus the "noise" the prior wants removed) and returns $x^*$; $F$ is fed $x^*-u^*$ and pulls back to $x^*$. Equal and opposite offsets, one common output: that is consensus. (Lecture 27)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/mcmc"] = [
  R`<b>Goal and inverse transform sampling.</b> Generate samples of $p(x)=\frac1ze^{-u(x)}$ without knowing $z$. The classic method $U\sim\mathrm{Uniform}[0,1]$, $X=F^{-1}(U)$ with $F^{-1}(u)=\inf\{x:F(x)\ge u\}$ works for any distribution, but for a Gibbs density (e.g. the bimodal $u(x)=4(x-1)^2(x+1)^2+x/2$) the CDF and its inverse have no closed form. Any strictly positive PMF can be written as Gibbs with $u=-\log p$. (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>Metropolis algorithm.</b> Symmetric proposal $q(w|x)=q(x|w)$ (e.g. $W=X+Z$ with $Z$ symmetric: $q(w|x)=p_Z(w-x)=p_Z(x-w)=q(x|w)$). Initialize $X^{(0)}$; repeat: draw $W\sim q(\cdot|X^{(k)})$; $\Delta E\leftarrow u(W)-u(X^{(k)})$; $\alpha\leftarrow\min\{1,e^{-\Delta E}\}$; set $X^{(k+1)}\leftarrow W$ with probability $\alpha$, else $X^{(k+1)}\leftarrow X^{(k)}$. Energy decreases are always accepted; increases are accepted with probability decaying exponentially in $\Delta E$. (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>Transition probabilities of the sampler.</b> For $x'\ne x$: $p(x'|x)=q(x'|x)\alpha(x'|x)$ (propose and accept); $p(x|x)=1-\sum_{w\ne x}q(w|x)\alpha(w|x)$. The samples form a homogeneous Markov chain because $X^{(k+1)}$ depends only on $X^{(k)}$ and fresh randomness. (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>Detailed-balance proof (Metropolis).</b> Show $p(x'|x)\frac1ze^{-u(x)}=p(x|x')\frac1ze^{-u(x')}$. Case 1, $u(x')\le u(x)$, $x'\ne x$: $p(x'|x)=q(x'|x)\cdot1=q(x|x')=q(x|x')e^{-[u(x)-u(x')]}e^{u(x)-u(x')}=q(x|x')\alpha(x|x')e^{u(x)-u(x')}=p(x|x')e^{u(x)-u(x')}$; rearrange. Case 2 ($u(x')>u(x)$) is the same with roles swapped; case 3 ($x'=x$) is trivial. Summing DBE over $x$ gives FBE, so $p$ is stationary. (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>When the chain is ergodic.</b> Add: irreducible (the proposal can reach every state), aperiodic (any positive rejection probability gives $P_{x,x}>0$), finite state space; then by the limit theorem $\lim_kP\{X^{(k)}=x\}=\frac1ze^{-u(x)}$. In practice the method is used for continuous high-dimensional MRFs too. (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>Hastings–Metropolis.</b> Any proposal $q(w|x)$, with the asymmetry corrected in $\alpha(w|x)=\min\Big\{1,\dfrac{q(x|w)}{q(w|x)}e^{-[u(w)-u(x)]}\Big\}=\min\Big\{1,\dfrac{q(x|w)\,p(w)}{q(w|x)\,p(x)}\Big\}$; same accept/reject step. Detailed balance is proved by the same three cases (case 1: the ratio $\ge1$ so $\alpha=1$; multiply by the two exponentials and recognize $\alpha(x|x')$). Special case $q(w|x)=p(w)$: $\alpha\equiv1$ and the chain is exact in one step. (Lecture 40)`,
  R`<b>Why proposals matter.</b> Rejections leave the state unchanged and are wasted; after burn-in the samples are still correlated, so slow mixing means many more samples per effectively independent draw. Proposal-variance trade-off: too small gives high acceptance but tiny moves, too large gives many rejections. Hastings–Metropolis lets you bias proposals toward the target (e.g. a Gaussian mixture approximating a bimodal density), sharply reducing repeated samples. (Lecture 40)`,
  R`<b>Coordinatewise sampling of an MRF.</b> Proposal $q_s(w|x)=g_s(w_s|x)\,\delta(w_r-x_r;\,r\ne s)$ changes one pixel; visit pixels in a fixed order $s_0,\dots,s_{N-1}$; acceptance $\alpha=\min\{1,\frac{g_s(x_s|w)}{g_s(w_s|x)}e^{-[u(w)-u(x)]}\}$ where only the cliques containing $s$ enter $u(w)-u(x)$. The per-update chain is not homogeneous, but the sweep-subsampled chain $Y^{(k)}=X^{(Nk)}$ is; if $g_s>0$ it is irreducible and aperiodic, DBE holds for each update, so $Y^{(k)}\to\frac1ze^{-u}$. (Lecture 40)`,
  R`<b>Gibbs sampler.</b> Use the pixel's own conditional as the proposal: $g_s(x_s|x_{\partial s})=P\{X_s=x_s|X_{r\ne s}\}=\dfrac{e^{-u_s(x_s|x_{\partial s})}}{\sum_{x'_s}e^{-u_s(x'_s|x_{\partial s})}}$ with $u_s=\sum_{c\in C_s}V_c(x_c)$ (cliques containing $s$; the others cancel). Then $\alpha=\min\Big\{1,\frac{g_s(x_s|\cdot)}{g_s(w_s|\cdot)}\cdot\frac{e^{-u_s(w_s|\cdot)}}{e^{-u_s(x_s|\cdot)}}\Big\}=1$: every proposal is accepted. Algorithm: for $L$ sweeps, for each pixel, draw $W_s\sim g_s(\cdot|X_{\partial s})$ and set $X_s\leftarrow W_s$. (Lecture 41)`,
  R`<b>Stationarity of the random-site Gibbs sampler (direct proof).</b> Pick $J$ uniformly from $M$ sites and redraw $x_J$. For $x'$ differing from $x$ only at $J$, $P(x\to x')=\frac1Mp_J(x'_J|x_{\setminus J})$. Then $\sum_xp(x)P(x\to x')=\frac1M\sum_Jp_J(x'_J|x'_{\setminus J})\sum_{x_J}p(x_J,x'_{\setminus J})=\frac1M\sum_Jp_J(x'_J|x'_{\setminus J})\,p(x'_{\setminus J})=\frac1M\sum_Jp(x')=p(x')$. Finite state space $K^M$, irreducible (change one coordinate at a time), aperiodic (redrawing the same value has positive probability), hence ergodic with stationary $p$; it is also reversible since $p(x)P(x\to x')=\frac1Mp(x_{\setminus J})p(x_J|\cdot)p(x'_J|\cdot)$ is symmetric in $x,x'$.`,
  R`<b>Ising example.</b> $g_s(x_s|x_{\partial s})\propto\exp\{-\beta\sum_{r\in\partial s}\delta(x_s\ne x_r)\}$; for binary states $P\{X_s=1|\cdot\}=\big(1+e^{2\beta[v(1)-2]}\big)^{-1}$ with $v(1)$ the number of neighbors not equal to 1. A $16\times16$ simulation at $\beta=1>\beta_c\approx0.88$ drifts to nearly all $+1$ or all $-1$: the distribution is bimodal above the critical value. (Lectures 38, 41)`,
  R`<b>Convergence caveats and annealing.</b> Guaranteed convergence says nothing about speed: large images or large $\beta$ can trap the sampler in a deep mode for impractically long. Simulated annealing samples $p_T\propto e^{-u/T}$ while lowering $T\to0$ so the samples concentrate on the MAP estimate. Ergodicity lets posterior expectations be computed as time averages of samples (stochastic integration, stochastic EM). (Lectures 39, 41)`,
  R`<b>Score-based samplers (GPnP notes).</b> Langevin dynamics $X_n=X_{n-1}+\epsilon\,s(X_{n-1})+\sqrt{2\epsilon}\,W_n$ with score $s=\nabla\log p=-\nabla u$; denoising score matching gives $s_\sigma(\tilde x)=\frac{1}{\sigma^2}(\text{Denoise}(\tilde x;\sigma)-\tilde x)$ (Tweedie), so with $\epsilon=\sigma^2$ each step is "denoise, then add noise of variance $2\sigma^2$", annealed with decreasing $\sigma$. GPnP alternates a prior proximal generator $(1-r\beta)x+r\beta\,\text{Denoise}(x;\sigma)+\sqrt\beta\sigma W$ with a forward-model generator $\bar F_1(x)+\sqrt\beta\sigma W$; its stationary distribution is the posterior with a Gaussian-blurred prior.`,
  R`<b>Exam checklist for a sampler question.</b> (1) Write $p\propto e^{-u}$ and identify $u$. (2) State the proposal and prove symmetry if Metropolis. (3) Write $\alpha$ (with the $q$-ratio if Hastings). (4) Write $P_{i,j}=q\alpha$ for $i\ne j$. (5) Prove DBE with $p$ by the two cases, conclude FBE. (6) Argue irreducible, aperiodic, finite $\Rightarrow$ ergodic, so the samples converge to $p$.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/mlfinal"] = [
  R`<b>Sufficient statistics.</b> $T(Y)$ is sufficient for $\{p(y|\theta)\}$ if $p(y|\theta)=h(y)\,g(T(y),\theta)$ (Fisher–Neyman factorization). Then $\hat\theta_{ML}=\arg\max_\theta\log g(T(y),\theta)=f(T(y))$ depends on the data only through $T$. Bernoulli: $T=N_1$; Gaussian: $T=(b,S)$ with $b=\sum y_n$, $S=\sum y_n^2$ (or $\sum y_ny_n^t$). (Lectures 3, 33)`,
  R`<b>Exponential family.</b> A $k$-parameter exponential family has $p(y|\theta)=\exp\{\langle\eta(\theta),T(y)\rangle+d(\theta)+s(y)\}$; $T$ is the natural sufficient statistic. To prove membership: take the log and separate it into (function of $\theta$)$\times$(statistic of $y$) + (function of $\theta$ only) + (function of $y$ only). ML: $\hat\theta=\arg\max\{\langle\eta(\theta),T\rangle+d(\theta)\}$. (Lecture 33)`,
  R`<b>Multinomial is an exponential family.</b> i.i.d. labels $X_n\in\{0,\dots,M-1\}$, $P\{X_n=k\}=\theta_k$, counts $N_k$: $p(x|\theta)=\prod_k\theta_k^{N_k}=\exp\{\sum_kN_k\log\theta_k\}$, so $\eta_k(\theta)=\log\theta_k$, $T_k=N_k$, $d=0$, $s=0$ (for the count vector alone, $s$ is the log multinomial coefficient). ML: maximize $\sum_kN_k\log\theta_k+\lambda(1-\sum_k\theta_k)$; $N_k/\theta_k=\lambda\Rightarrow\theta_k=N_k/\lambda$; the constraint gives $\lambda=N$, so $\hat\theta_k=N_k/N$. (Lecture 33)`,
  R`<b>The simplex.</b> $\Omega=\{\theta\in\mathbb R^M:\theta_k\ge0,\ \sum_k\theta_k=1\}$ is the (probability) simplex, an $(M-1)$-dimensional set. Convex: a convex combination of two probability vectors is nonnegative and sums to one. Closed: intersection of the closed half-spaces $\theta_k\ge0$ and the closed hyperplane $\sum\theta_k=1$. Bounded: $0\le\theta_k\le1$. Hence compact, so the continuous log-likelihood attains its maximum. $-\sum_kN_k\log\theta_k$ is strictly convex on the interior when all $N_k>0$ (Hessian $\operatorname{diag}(N_k/\theta_k^2)\succ0$), so the ML estimate is unique; if some $N_k=0$ the maximizer lies on the boundary.`,
  R`<b>i.i.d. Gaussian with unknown mean and variance.</b> $l(\theta)=\sum_n\big[\frac{(y_n-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}+\tfrac12\log(2\pi\sigma^2)\big]=\frac{S-2b\mu+N\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}+\frac N2\log(2\pi\sigma^2)$. Minimize over $\mu$ first: $\partial/\partial\mu$ gives $\hat\mu=b/N$ for every $\sigma^2$. Substitute and set $\partial/\partial\sigma^2=0$: $-\frac{S-b^2/N}{2\sigma^4}+\frac{N}{2\sigma^2}=0\Rightarrow\hat\sigma^2=\frac SN-\big(\frac bN\big)^2$. Natural parameters $\eta=[\mu/\sigma^2,-1/(2\sigma^2)]$; $\hat\sigma^2$ is biased by the factor $(N-1)/N$. (Lecture 33)`,
  R`<b>Multivariate Gaussian.</b> $b=\sum x_k$, $S=\sum x_kx_k^t$; $\log p=-\frac n2\log|R|-\frac12\operatorname{tr}(SR^{-1})+b^tR^{-1}\mu-\frac n2\mu^tR^{-1}\mu$; $\hat\mu=b/n$, $\hat R=S/n-\hat\mu\hat\mu^t$ (via $\partial\log|R|/\partial R=R^{-1}$, $\partial\operatorname{tr}(SR^{-1})/\partial R=-R^{-1}SR^{-1}$, or the eigenvalue argument $\sum(\lambda_i-\log\lambda_i)$ minimized at $\lambda_i=1$). (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>Poisson and exponential.</b> Poisson $p(y|\lambda)=\lambda^ye^{-\lambda}/y!=\exp\{y\log\lambda-\lambda-\log y!\}$: $\eta=\log\lambda$, $T=\sum y_n$, $\hat\lambda=\bar y$, unbiased with variance $\lambda/N$. Exponential $p(y|\mu)=\frac1\mu e^{-y/\mu}=\exp\{-y/\mu-\log\mu\}$: $\eta=-1/\mu$, $T=\sum y_n$, $\hat\mu=\bar y$, unbiased ($E[Y]=\mu$), variance $\mu^2/N$, MSE $=\mu^2/N$. In rate form $\hat\lambda=1/\bar y$ is biased.`,
  R`<b>Frequentist quality measures.</b> $\text{bias}=E_\theta[\hat\theta]-\theta$, $\text{var}=E_\theta[(\hat\theta-E\hat\theta)^2]$, $\text{MSE}=\text{var}+\text{bias}^2$, all functions of $\theta$. Unbiased, UMVU, consistent ($P\{|\hat\theta_n-\theta|>\epsilon\}\to0$). ML is generally biased but consistent and asymptotically efficient (Cramér–Rao). A constant estimator has zero MSE at one $\theta$, so MSE alone does not rank estimators. (Lecture 4)`,
  R`<b>Convexity of the negative log-likelihood.</b> In the natural parameter $\eta$, $-\log p=-\langle\eta,T\rangle+A(\eta)-s(y)$ where $A(\eta)=\log\int e^{\langle\eta,T(y)\rangle+s(y)}dy=-d$ is convex (log-sum-exp), so the NLL is convex in $\eta$ and any stationary point is the global ML estimate; strict convexity when $T$ is not degenerate. For Poisson, $-\log p=N\lambda-T\log\lambda+c$ has second derivative $T/\lambda^2>0$.`,
  R`<b>Markov chains and mixtures are exponential families.</b> Chain: $\log p(x)=\sum_jN_j\log\tau_j+\sum_{i,j}K_{i,j}\log P_{i,j}$, $\hat P_{i,j}=K_{i,j}/\sum_jK_{i,j}$. Mixture complete data $(Y,X)$: $T=(N_m,b_m,S_m)$, giving $\hat\pi_m=N_m/N$, $\hat\mu_m=b_m/N_m$, $\hat R_m=S_m/N_m-b_mb_m^t/N_m^2$; EM replaces $T$ by $E[T|y,\theta']$. (Lectures 33, 34)`,
  R`<b>ML for an AR process.</b> $\hat h=\hat R^{-1}\hat b$, $\hat\sigma_C^2=\hat\sigma_x^2-\hat b^t\hat R^{-1}\hat b$ from the three sample statistics; one pass and one linear solve. (Lecture 7)`,
  R`<b>ML of a Gibbs scale parameter.</b> $z(\sigma)=\sigma^Nz_0$ so $\hat\sigma=\arg\min\{u(x/\sigma)+N\log\sigma\}$; homogeneous energy of degree $p$: $\hat\sigma^p=\frac pNu(x)$, e.g. GGMRF $\hat\sigma^p=\frac1N\sum b_{s,r}|x_s-x_r|^p$. The shape/temperature parameters need $\log z(\theta)$ and are hard. (Lecture 16)`,
  R`<b>ML versus MAP.</b> MAP adds $-\log p(x)$; ML is its flat-prior limit; Bayesian estimators trade bias for variance and need a prior, ML needs none but needs data $\gg$ unknowns. (Lecture 4)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/mmfinal"] = [
  R`<b>Definition.</b> $q(x;x')$ majorizes $f$ at $x'$ if $q(x';x')=f(x')$ and $f(x)\le q(x;x')$ for all $x$; equivalently $Q$ is a surrogate if $f(x)\le Q(x;x')-Q(x';x')+f(x')$ (a shift depending only on $x'$ is allowed). For maximization (EM) the inequality is reversed. (Lecture 19)`,
  R`<b>MM iteration and monotonicity proof.</b> $x^{(k+1)}=\arg\min_xQ(x;x^{(k)})$ and $f(x^{(k+1)})\le q(x^{(k+1)};x^{(k)})\le q(x^{(k)};x^{(k)})=f(x^{(k)})$; bounded-below $f$ gives a convergent cost sequence (iterates need not converge). Any $x$ with $Q(x;x')&lt;Q(x';x')$ already lowers $f$. (Lecture 19)`,
  R`<b>Fixed-point proof.</b> If $Q,f$ are $C^1$ and $x^*\in\operatorname{int}(A)$ minimizes $Q(\cdot;x^*)$, then $0=\nabla Q(x;x^*)|_{x=x^*}=\nabla f(x^*)$ by the gradient (tangency) property, so $x^*$ is a stationary point, a global minimum when $f$ is convex. Proof of the gradient property: $r(x)=Q(x;x')-Q(x';x')+f(x')-f(x)\ge0$ with $r(x')=0$, so $x'$ minimizes $r$ and $\nabla r(x')=0$. (Lecture 19)`,
  R`<b>Additivity and composition.</b> Sums of surrogates majorize sums; $Q(g(x);g(x'))$ majorizes $f(g(x))$. These turn a 1-D surrogate $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')$ into the MAP surrogate $Q(x;x')=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\sum_{\{s,r\}}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r;x'_s-x'_r)$. (Lecture 19)`,
  R`<b>Maximum-curvature surrogate.</b> $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')=\rho'(\Delta')\Delta+\frac{c_{\max}}{2}(\Delta-\Delta')^2$, $c_{\max}=\max\rho''$. Simple but loose, and undefined when $\rho''$ is unbounded. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Symmetric-bound surrogate.</b> $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')=\dfrac{\rho'(\Delta')}{2\Delta'}\Delta^2$ (+ const), curvature $\rho''(0)$ at $\Delta'=0$; valid when $\rho'(\Delta)/\Delta$ is non-increasing in $|\Delta|$ (influence function increasing near 0, concave for $\Delta>0$). It touches $\rho$ at $\pm\Delta'$ and has smaller curvature than $c_{\max}$ for large $|\Delta'|$, so steps are larger. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Huber example (2017).</b> $\rho(\Delta)=\Delta^2/2$ for $|\Delta|&lt;T$, $T|\Delta|-T^2/2$ otherwise; $\rho'(\Delta)=\operatorname{clip}\{\Delta,[-T,T]\}$, so $\rho'(\Delta')/\Delta'=1$ inside and $T/|\Delta'|$ outside: surrogate curvature $\min(1,T/|\Delta'|)$, i.e. $\rho(\Delta;\Delta')=\tfrac12\min(1,T/|\Delta'|)\Delta^2+\text{const}$. Verify the upper bound: inside it is exact, outside $\frac{T}{2|\Delta'|}\Delta^2\ge T|\Delta|-T^2/2$ with equality at $|\Delta|=|\Delta'|$ (AM–GM). (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Reweighted quadratic form.</b> $\tilde b_{s,r}\leftarrow b_{s,r}\rho'(x'_s-x'_r)/(2(x'_s-x'_r))$ and $Q=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2+\sum\tilde b_{s,r}(x_s-x_r)^2$; the line search becomes $\alpha^*=\operatorname{clip}\{-\theta_1/\theta_2,[a,b]\}$ and ICD uses $\theta_1=-e^t\Lambda A_{*,s}+\sum_{r\in\partial s}2\tilde b_{s,r}(x_s-x_r)$, $\theta_2=A_{*,s}^t\Lambda A_{*,s}+\sum_{r\in\partial s}2\tilde b_{s,r}$. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>EM as majorization.</b> $\log p(y|\theta)\ge Q(\theta;\theta')-Q(\theta';\theta')+\log p(y|\theta')$, so $Q$ is a surrogate for maximizing the likelihood; monotone likelihood, and the EM fixed-point theorem ($\nabla\log p(y|\theta^*)=0$, needing $\Omega$ open) is exactly the fixed-point property. Sketch: $Q(\theta;\theta')+H(\theta';\theta')$ touches $\log p(y|\theta)$ at $\theta'$ from below. (Lectures 30, 32)`,
  R`<b>Which potentials work.</b> Bounded $\rho''(0)$ (quadratic, Huber, log cosh, QGGMRF with $q=2$) admit both surrogates; total variation and GGMRF with $1\le p&lt;2$ have infinite curvature at zero, so use proximal/ADMM methods instead. (Lecture 20)`,
  R`<b>Separable data-term surrogate.</b> Exact Taylor expansion of $\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|^2$ about $x'$, then replace $A^tA$ by a diagonal $D\succeq A^tA$ (e.g. $\lambda_{\max}I$ or row-sum bound); minimizer $x'-D^{-1}A^t(Ax'-y)$ is a parallel, pixel-separable update.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/mrf"] = [
  R`<b>Neighborhood systems and cliques.</b> $\partial:S\to2^S$ is a neighborhood system if $r\in\partial s\Rightarrow s\in\partial r$ and $s\notin\partial s$. A clique $c\subset S$ is a set of points that are pairwise neighbors (single points count; for the 8-point system there are 1-, 2-, 3- and 4-point cliques). Cliques and neighborhoods carry the same information; the minimal neighborhood induced by cliques is $\partial s=\{r\ne s:\exists c\ni r,s\}$. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>MRF definition.</b> $X$ on $S$ is an MRF if $P\{X_s=x_s|X_r,r\ne s\}=P\{X_s=x_s|X_{\partial s}\}$ for all $s$. Pitfall: $p(x)\ne\prod_sp(x_s|x_{\partial s})$ in general because of loopy dependencies; the joint must come from a Gibbs distribution. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Gibbs distribution.</b> $p(x)=\frac1z\exp\{-\sum_{c\in C}V_c(x_c)\}$ with potentials $V_c$ on cliques, energy $u(x)=\sum_cV_c(x_c)$, partition function $z=\sum_xe^{-u(x)}$. Pairwise form: $u=\sum_{\{s,r\}\in P}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)$. Physical origin: maximum entropy at fixed expected energy gives $\frac1ze^{-u/(k_BT)}$. (Lectures 15, 38)`,
  R`<b>Hammersley–Clifford.</b> If $p(x)>0$, $X$ is an MRF with neighborhood $\partial$ iff $p$ is a Gibbs distribution on the cliques of $\partial$. The Gibbs $\Rightarrow$ MRF direction is constructive; the converse is existential (it tells you a $u$ exists, not $z$). Practical use: specify the model by writing $u(x)$, then read off the conditionals. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Conditional of a pixel from a Gibbs distribution.</b> Split $C=C_s\cup\bar C_s$ (cliques containing $s$ or not): $P\{X_s=x_s|X_{r\ne s}\}=\dfrac{\frac1ze^{-\sum_{C_s}V_c}e^{-\sum_{\bar C_s}V_c}}{\sum_{x_s}\frac1ze^{-\sum_{C_s}V_c}e^{-\sum_{\bar C_s}V_c}}=\dfrac{e^{-u_s(x_s|x_{\partial s})}}{\sum_{x'_s}e^{-u_s(x'_s|x_{\partial s})}}$ with $u_s=\sum_{c\in C_s}V_c$. Both $z$ and the cliques not containing $s$ cancel; the result depends only on neighbors. Continuous case: replace the sum by $\int dx'_s$. (Lectures 15, 38)`,
  R`<b>Ising model.</b> Spins $x_s\in\{-1,+1\}$, 4-point neighborhood, pair energy $-Jx_sx_r$; Boltzmann $p\propto\exp\{\frac{J}{k_BT}\sum x_rx_s\}$. Using $x_rx_s=1-2\delta(x_r\ne x_s)$: $p(x)=\frac{1}{z(\beta)}\exp\{-\beta\sum_{\{r,s\}}\delta(x_r\ne x_s)\}$ with $\beta=2J/(k_BT)$ (inverse temperature). The energy is $\beta\times$(total boundary length). (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Ising conditional.</b> With $v(x_s,x_{\partial s})=\sum_{r\in\partial s}\delta(x_s\ne x_r)$ (number of disagreeing neighbors), $p(x_s|x_{\partial s})=\dfrac{e^{-\beta v(x_s,x_{\partial s})}}{e^{-\beta v(1,x_{\partial s})}+e^{-\beta v(-1,x_{\partial s})}}$; since $v(-1)=4-v(1)$, $P\{X_s=1|x_{\partial s}\}=\dfrac{1}{1+e^{2\beta[v(1,x_{\partial s})-2]}}$. Half the neighbors each way gives $1/2$. $\beta>0$: majority vote; $\beta=0$ ($T=\infty$): independent; $\beta&lt;0$: anti-aligned/checkerboard. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Critical temperature.</b> In 2-D, for $\beta>\beta_c\approx0.88$ correlations persist at infinite distance: $\lim_NP\{X_0=-1|X_B=1\}&lt;1/2$ (Peierls; Kindermann–Snell proof bounds the probability by $\sum_L|C_L|e^{-\beta L}$ with $|C_L|\le4L^23^L$). Onsager: $\lim E[X_0|B=1]=(1-\sinh^{-4}\beta)^{1/8}$ for $\beta>\beta_c$, $0$ below. The distribution is bimodal (nearly all $+1$ or all $-1$); the boundary breaks the $x\leftrightarrow-x$ symmetry. In 1-D no such behavior exists. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>M-level (Potts) MRF.</b> $x_s\in\{0,\dots,M-1\}$, 8-point neighborhood with $t_1$ = number of disagreeing horizontal/vertical pairs and $t_2$ = diagonal pairs: $p(x)=\frac1z\exp\{-\beta_1t_1(x)-\beta_2t_2(x)\}$; conditional $p(x_s|x_{\partial s})\propto\exp\{-\beta_1v_1(x_s,x_{\partial s})-\beta_2v_2(x_s,x_{\partial s})\}$, normalized over the $M$ values. Positive $\beta$s favor the majority class of the neighbors. (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Continuous pairwise priors: choice of $\rho$.</b> Conditional of a pixel: $p(x_s|x_{\partial s})\propto\exp\{-\sum_{r\in\partial s}b_{s,r}\rho(x_s-x_r)\}$, most probable value where the forces balance, $\sum_rb_{s,r}\rho'(\hat x_s-x_r)=0$. Quadratic $\rho=\Delta^2/2$: Gaussian conditional with mean $\sum_rb_{s,r}x_r/\sum_rb_{s,r}$, closed-form MAP, but unbounded influence $\rho'=\Delta$ smooths edges. Absolute value $|\Delta|$: convex, edge-preserving, constant influence, but nondifferentiable at $0$ (flat "waxy" regions, ICD stalls, needs prox/ADMM), MAP no longer unique in general. $|\Delta|^p$, $1&lt;p&lt;2$ (GGMRF): strictly convex, differentiable, no threshold. Huber/QGGMRF: quadratic near 0, slower growth beyond $T$. Nonconvex (weak spring, Geman–McClure): sharpest edges but unstable, discontinuous MAP. (Lecture 16)`,
  R`<b>What the partition function permits.</b> $z$ is intractable (exponentially many states), which blocks ML of $\beta$; but MAP needs only $\arg\min u(x)$ and samplers only $p(x')/p(x)=e^{u(x)-u(x')}$. The scale parameter is the exception: $z(\sigma)=\sigma^Nz_0$. (Lectures 16, 38)`,
  R`<b>1-D versus 2-D.</b> A 1-D MRF of order $P$ with positive PMF is exactly a Markov chain of order $P$ (so MRFs add nothing in 1-D). In 2-D a Markov chain of order $P$ is an MRF of order $2P$ (autocorrelation of the causal window), but the Ising model is not a 2-D Markov chain of any finite order. Advantages of MRFs: isotropic, local; disadvantages: probability and parameter estimation are hard. (Lecture 38)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/icdl1"] = [
  R`<b>The cost and its convexity.</b> $f(x)=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|^2+\lambda\|x\|_1$ ($\Lambda=I$; in general $\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|_\Lambda^2$). The quadratic term is convex (strictly convex iff $A$ has full column rank, i.e. $A^tA\succ0$) and $\lambda\|x\|_1$ is convex, so $f$ is convex, and strictly convex when $A$ has rank $N$. Bayesian reading: MAP for $Y=AX+W$ with i.i.d. Laplacian prior $p(x)=(\lambda/2)^Ne^{-\lambda\|x\|_1}$. (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Existence and uniqueness.</b> $f$ is continuous and coercive because $\lambda\|x\|_1\to\infty$ as $\|x\|\to\infty$ (even if $A$ is rank-deficient), so every sublevel set is closed and bounded and a minimizer exists. Uniqueness follows from strict convexity (full column rank); otherwise the minimizer set is convex but may not be a single point.`,
  R`<b>Single-pixel cost.</b> Holding the other pixels fixed, with $x_{old}$ the current value, $e=y-Ax$, $\theta_1=-e^tA_{*,s}$ and $\theta_2=\|A_{*,s}\|^2$ (with $\Lambda$: $\theta_1=-e^t\Lambda A_{*,s}$, $\theta_2=A_{*,s}^t\Lambda A_{*,s}$): $f_s(x_s)=\theta_1(x_s-x_{old})+\tfrac12\theta_2(x_s-x_{old})^2+\lambda|x_s|+\text{const}$. Complete the square: $f_s=\tfrac12\theta_2\big(x_s-(x_{old}-\theta_1/\theta_2)\big)^2+\lambda|x_s|+\text{const}$. (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Closed-form ICD update via shrinkage.</b> Dividing by $\theta_2$ gives the L1 proximal problem $\arg\min_{x_s}\{\tfrac12(x_s-\tilde x)^2+\frac{\lambda}{\theta_2}|x_s|\}$ with $\tilde x=x_{old}-\theta_1/\theta_2=x_{old}+\frac{A_{*,s}^te}{\|A_{*,s}\|^2}$ (the unregularized least-squares minimizer), so $x_s\leftarrow S_{\lambda/\theta_2}(\tilde x)=\operatorname{sign}(\tilde x)\max\{|\tilde x|-\lambda/\|A_{*,s}\|^2,0\}$. Derivation of $S$: three cases $x_s>0$, $x_s&lt;0$ (set derivative to zero), and $x_s=0$ when neither is consistent, i.e. $|\tilde x|\le\lambda/\theta_2$. (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Pseudo-code.</b> Initialize $e\leftarrow y-Ax$. For $K$ iterations, for each pixel $s$: $x_{old}\leftarrow x_s$; $\theta_1\leftarrow-e^tA_{*,s}$; $\theta_2\leftarrow\|A_{*,s}\|^2$; $x_s\leftarrow S_{\lambda/\theta_2}(x_{old}-\theta_1/\theta_2)$; $e\leftarrow e-A_{*,s}(x_s-x_{old})$. Positivity can be added by clipping the shrunk value at zero (prox of $\lambda|x|+$ indicator). (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Why an ICD fixed point is a global minimum.</b> $f=g(x)+\sum_s\lambda|x_s|$ with $g$ smooth convex and the nonsmooth part <em>separable</em>. $x^*$ is a global minimizer iff $0\in\partial f(x^*)$, i.e. for every $s$: $\partial g/\partial x_s(x^*)+\lambda\operatorname{sign}(x^*_s)=0$ if $x^*_s\ne0$, and $|\partial g/\partial x_s(x^*)|\le\lambda$ if $x^*_s=0$. These are exactly the per-coordinate optimality conditions that a fixed point of the shrinkage update satisfies, so coordinatewise optimality equals global optimality. The nondifferentiable manifolds $\{x_s=0\}$ are aligned with the coordinate axes (Alliney–Ruzinsky 1994). (Lecture 23)`,
  R`<b>Where this breaks.</b> For $\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|^2+\|Bx\|_1$ (e.g. total variation) the kinks lie along $\{(Bx)_k=0\}$, not along coordinate axes, so ICD can stall at a non-minimizer; the ICD convergence theorem needs continuous differentiability. Use variable splitting/ADMM: $v=Bx$, quadratic $x$-step, $v\leftarrow S_{1/a}(Bx+u)$. (Lectures 23, 25)`,
  R`<b>Relation to proximal maps and LASSO.</b> With $A=I$ the whole update collapses to $x=S_\lambda(y)$, the proximal map of $\lambda\|\cdot\|_1$ (a MAP denoiser under a Laplacian prior). The same cost is the LASSO; the L1 term drives small coefficients exactly to zero (model selection / sparsity). (Lectures 16, 23)`,
  R`<b>Threshold scaling.</b> The effective threshold on pixel $s$ is $\lambda/\|A_{*,s}\|^2$: pixels the sensor barely observes (small column norm) are shrunk harder. With noise precision $\Lambda$, replace $\|A_{*,s}\|^2$ by $A_{*,s}^t\Lambda A_{*,s}$.`,
  R`<b>Comparison with the Gaussian prior.</b> Quadratic prior $\tfrac12\lambda\|x\|^2$ gives the linear update $x_s\leftarrow\frac{\theta_2}{\theta_2+\lambda}\tilde x$ (uniform attenuation, nothing set to zero); the L1 prior gives soft thresholding (zeros plus a constant shift). Both are exact 1-D minimizations, so no step size is needed and the cost is monotone.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/genrv"] = [
  R`<b>Inverse-CDF method.</b> Let $U\sim\mathrm{Uniform}[0,1]$ and $F$ be a continuous strictly increasing CDF. Then $X=F^{-1}(U)$ has CDF $F$. Proof: $P\{X\le\lambda\}=P\{F^{-1}(U)\le\lambda\}=P\{U\le F(\lambda)\}=F(\lambda)$, using that $F$ is increasing (so $F^{-1}(u)\le\lambda\Leftrightarrow u\le F(\lambda)$) and that $P\{U\le t\}=t$ on $[0,1]$. (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>General CDFs.</b> Define the generalized inverse $F^{-1}(u)=\inf\{x:F(x)\ge u\}$; then $X=F^{-1}(U)$ has CDF $F$ for <em>any</em> distribution, including discrete ones and CDFs with flat parts. Equivalently, if $X$ has continuous CDF $F$ then $F(X)\sim\mathrm{Uniform}[0,1]$ (probability integral transform).`,
  R`<b>Worked examples.</b> Exponential with mean $\mu$: $F(x)=1-e^{-x/\mu}$, so $X=-\mu\ln(1-U)$ (or $-\mu\ln U$). Uniform on $[a,b]$: $X=a+(b-a)U$. Rayleigh, Cauchy ($X=\tan(\pi(U-\tfrac12))$), and any invertible closed-form CDF work the same way.`,
  R`<b>Discrete distributions.</b> For $P\{X=k\}=p_k$, compute cumulative sums $c_k=\sum_{j\le k}p_j$ and set $X=\min\{k:U\le c_k\}$; this is the generalized inverse. Bernoulli($\theta$): $X=1$ iff $U&lt;\theta$. Multinomial labels in EM/Gibbs sampling are generated this way.`,
  R`<b>Gaussians.</b> $\Phi^{-1}$ has no closed form, but pairs of independent Gaussians have a squared norm that is exponential: with $R^2=-2\ln U_1$ and $\Theta=2\pi U_2$, $X_1=R\cos\Theta$, $X_2=R\sin\Theta$ are i.i.d. $N(0,1)$ (Box–Muller). Vector $N(\mu,R)$: $X=\mu+R^{1/2}W$ with $W\sim N(0,I)$ and $R^{1/2}=E\Lambda^{1/2}E^t$ (or a Cholesky factor). AR/GMRF samples: filter white noise or solve $B^{1/2}X=W$. (Lectures 7, 39)`,
  R`<b>Change of variables check.</b> For monotone $g$, $X=g(U)$ has density $p_X(x)=p_U(g^{-1}(x))\,|d g^{-1}/dx|=\frac{d}{dx}F(x)$ when $g=F^{-1}$, confirming the density is $F'$. Use this to verify any proposed transformation.`,
  R`<b>Where it fails.</b> For Gibbs densities $p(x)=\frac1ze^{-u(x)}$ (e.g. bimodal $u(x)=4(x-1)^2(x+1)^2+x/2$, or any MRF) neither $F$ nor $F^{-1}$ nor even $z$ is available, and in high dimension the CDF is not even defined usefully; this motivates Metropolis, Hastings–Metropolis and Gibbs sampling, which need only energy differences. (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>Use inside MCMC.</b> Proposals are drawn by inverse transform, $W\leftarrow Q^{-1}(U|X^{(k)})$, and the accept/reject step compares a second uniform with $\alpha$: $X^{(k+1)}\leftarrow W$ if $U&lt;\alpha$. The Gibbs sampler draws each pixel from its discrete conditional by cumulative sums. (Lectures 39, 41)`,
  R`<b>Symmetric proposals.</b> $W=X+Z$ with $Z$ having a symmetric density gives $q(w|x)=p_Z(w-x)=q(x|w)$, the condition Metropolis requires; generate $Z$ by the inverse-CDF method as well.`,
  R`<b>Uniform random numbers.</b> Everything rests on a pseudo-random generator for $\mathrm{Uniform}[0,1]$; independent draws $U_1,U_2,\dots$ give independent samples, and a bad generator corrupts every downstream distribution.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece641/final/wordsfinal"] = [
  R`<b>How to answer.</b> Identify each symbol, say what each side of the equation represents, explain the balance or flow it expresses, and give the consequence (what it guarantees, what breaks without it). Aim for accurate plain language a non-expert could follow; the notes call this the "emotional" interpretation.`,
  R`<b>Detailed balance</b> $\pi_iP_{i,j}=\pi_jP_{j,i}$: "in steady state, the amount of probability moving from $i$ to $j$ in one step equals the amount moving back. Every pair of states trades evenly, so the chain run backwards is statistically identical to the chain run forwards. If you can find a $\pi$ satisfying it, summing over $i$ shows $\pi$ is stationary, which is how samplers are designed." (Lecture 37)`,
  R`<b>Full balance</b> $\pi_j=\sum_i\pi_iP_{i,j}$: "the probability flowing into state $j$ from everywhere equals the probability already there; nothing accumulates or drains, so one more step leaves the distribution unchanged. Detailed balance is pairwise bookkeeping, full balance is total bookkeeping." (Lecture 36)`,
  R`<b>Ergodic</b> $\pi_j=\lim_n[P^n]_{i,j}>0$: "wherever the chain starts, it forgets its origin and settles into the same distribution; because of that, watching one long run tells you the same thing as averaging over many independent runs." (Lecture 36)`,
  R`<b>The Q function</b> $Q(\theta;\theta')=E[\log p(y,X|\theta)|y,\theta']$: "the log-likelihood we would have if the labels were known, averaged over what the current model believes the labels are. It touches the true log-likelihood at the current parameter and lies below it elsewhere, so climbing $Q$ is guaranteed to climb the likelihood." (Lecture 30)`,
  R`<b>E-step and M-step</b>: "the E-step softly classifies every sample, giving it fractional membership in each class according to the current model; the M-step refits each class to its fractional members with ordinary ML formulas. Repeat, and the likelihood never goes down." (Lecture 32)`,
  R`<b>Metropolis acceptance</b> $\alpha=\min\{1,e^{-[u(w)-u(x)]}\}$: "always move downhill in energy; move uphill only sometimes, with a probability that shrinks exponentially with the climb. The willingness to go uphill is what lets the sampler explore the whole distribution instead of getting stuck in a valley." (Lecture 39)`,
  R`<b>Hastings correction</b> $\frac{q(x|w)}{q(w|x)}$: "if the proposal mechanism prefers to suggest $w$ from $x$ more than $x$ from $w$, that preference would bias the samples; dividing by it cancels the bias, so any proposal can be used and the target is still the stationary distribution." (Lecture 40)`,
  R`<b>Gibbs sampler</b> $X_s\sim p(x_s|x_{\partial s})$: "replace one pixel at a time with a fresh draw from what its neighbors say it should be; because the draw already has the right conditional distribution, nothing is ever rejected, and after enough sweeps the whole image is a sample from the model." (Lecture 41)`,
  R`<b>Partition function</b> $z=\sum_xe^{-u(x)}$: "the normalizing constant that makes the Gibbs formula a probability. It sums over every possible image, so it is uncomputable, but it does not depend on which image we are looking at, so comparing images, finding the most probable one, and sampling all work without it." (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Consensus equilibrium</b> $x^*=F(x^*-u^*)=H(x^*+u^*)$: "the sensor agent and the prior agent each receive the shared image nudged by equal and opposite offsets and both return the shared image; neither is minimizing anything, they simply agree. That agreement replaces optimization as the definition of the solution when a learned denoiser stands in for the prior." (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Reflection operator</b> $T=(2H-I)(2F-I)$: "reflect through the sensor agent, then through the prior agent; a point that lands on itself is a consensus solution. Reflections never expand distances, so averaging each step with the previous point (Mann iteration) walks to a fixed point." (Lecture 27)`,
  R`<b>Inverse temperature</b> $\beta$ in the Ising model: "how strongly neighboring pixels want to agree. Zero means independent noise; small positive means gentle smoothing; past the critical value the whole image locks into one of two phases and the influence of the boundary never fades, no matter how large the image." (Lecture 38)`,
  R`<b>Tweedie's formula</b> $E[X|\tilde X]=\tilde X+\sigma^2\nabla\log p_\sigma(\tilde X)$: "the best denoiser takes the noisy image and steps uphill along the gradient of the log-density of noisy images; so training a denoiser by least squares secretly learns that gradient, the score, which is what generative samplers need." (GPnP notes)`
];

})();
